{"id": "36", "pred": "Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add vegetables and cheese before baking.", "raw_output": "To cook pollock, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\n2. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n3. Place the fillets on a baking sheet, optionally spray the sheet with butter-flavored cooking spray.\n4. Optionally, layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\n5. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish is cooked and flakes easily.\n6. For a cheesy variation, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to melt the cheese.\n\nAnswer: Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add vegetables and cheese before baking.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to cook pollock:\n\nPassage 1:\n\n* Spray a baking sheet with butter-flavored cooking spray.\n* Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n* Place the fillets on the baking sheet and layer them with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\n* Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n\nPassage 2:\n\n* Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\n* Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n* Bake the fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n\nPassage 3:\n\n* Bake the pollock fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily.\n* Turn off the oven and place two slices of cheese on top of each fillet.\n* Return the fish to the oven and allow the cheese to melt for about 3 minutes.\n\nTherefore, to cook pollock, you can follow these steps:\n\n1. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n2. Place the fillets on a baking sheet or in the oven.\n3. Cook the pollock until it flakes easily, either by baking it in the oven or on the stovetop.\n4. Add cheese to the pollock if desired and return it to the oven to melt the cheese.", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 32.929, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 21.69, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "37", "pred": "Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add vegetables and cheese before baking.", "raw_output": "To cook pollock, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\n2. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n3. Place the fillets on a baking sheet, optionally spray the sheet with butter-flavored cooking spray.\n4. Optionally, layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\n5. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish is cooked and flakes easily.\n6. For a cheesy variation, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to melt the cheese.\n\nAnswer: Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add vegetables and cheese before baking.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can cook pollock:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C) (Passage 2).\n2. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper (Passage 1 and 2).\n3. Place the pollock fillets on a baking sheet lined with butter-flavored cooking spray (Passage 1).\n4. Add onion, green pepper, and tomato slices to the baking sheet (Passage 1).\n5. Bake the pollock fillets in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish has cooked and flakes easily (Passage 1 and 2).\n6. To add cheesy flavor, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return the fish to the oven for an additional 3 minutes (Passage 3).\n\nNote that Passage 3 does not provide any specific instructions for cooking the pollock, but rather suggests adding cheese to the existing dish.", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 32.829, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.48, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "38", "pred": "Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add vegetables and cheese before baking.", "raw_output": "To cook pollock, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees F (175 degrees C).\n2. Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper.\n3. Place the fillets on a baking sheet, optionally spray the sheet with butter-flavored cooking spray.\n4. Optionally, layer the fillets with onion, green pepper, and tomato slices.\n5. Bake in the preheated oven for about 15 minutes, or until the fish is cooked and flakes easily.\n6. For a cheesy variation, turn off the oven, place two slices of cheese on each fillet, and return to the oven for about 3 minutes to melt the cheese.\n\nAnswer: Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C), season pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper, and bake for about 15 minutes until cooked and flakes easily. Optionally, add vegetables and cheese before baking.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to cook pollock:\n\nPreheat the oven to 350°F (175°C). Season the pollock fillets with paprika and black pepper. Place the fillets on a baking sheet lined with parchment paper or aluminum foil. You can also add sliced onions, green peppers, and tomatoes on top of the fillets. Bake for 15 minutes or until the fish flakes easily with a fork. If desired, top with cheese and return to the oven for an additional 3 minutes or until the cheese is melted.\n\nTherefore, the answer is not \"Unable to answer based on given passages.\"", "question": "how to cook pollock?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 32.875, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.68, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "39", "pred": "Note: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "raw_output": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to carefully scrape the softened plastic off, avoiding damage to the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nAlternatively, if the plastic is stubborn:\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, then attempt to peel it off.\n- If the plastic cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the dryer drum or the entire dryer, and consult a service technician for this repair.\n\nNote: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "gt": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, you can run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. Be careful not to damage the dryer's interior. You can then apply 1 to 2 tsp. of nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Afterward, rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover. You can mix 1 tsp. of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water, dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water. Some plastic may be extremely difficult to remove, in which case you can try heating the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun and then peel it off. If not all plastic comes off, you may need to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 38.567, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.5, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
