{"id": "42", "pred": "Note: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "raw_output": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to carefully scrape the softened plastic off, avoiding damage to the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nAlternatively, if the plastic is stubborn:\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, then attempt to peel it off.\n- If the plastic cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the dryer drum or the entire dryer, and consult a service technician for this repair.\n\nNote: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic, then scrape it off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper. (Passage 1)\n2. Heat the plastic with a hair dryer or heat gun to make it easier to peel off. Once heated, use a cloth to peel off the plastic. (Passage 2)\n3. Soak a cloth in an acetone-based nail polish remover and use it to scrub the remaining plastic off of the element after leaving it on the element for 5 minutes. Then, wipe the coils with a clean, damp cloth and remove any remaining residue with a cloth dipped in a solution of warm water and dish soap. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on the given passages. The passages do not provide specific instructions on how to remove plastic from the drum of a clothes dryer.", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 38.552, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "43", "pred": "Note: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "raw_output": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to carefully scrape the softened plastic off, avoiding damage to the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nAlternatively, if the plastic is stubborn:\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, then attempt to peel it off.\n- If the plastic cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the dryer drum or the entire dryer, and consult a service technician for this repair.\n\nNote: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to get plastic out of a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n4. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic is still difficult to remove, you can try heating it with a hair dryer or heat gun before attempting to scrape it off. However, if the plastic cannot be removed completely, it may be necessary to replace the drum or the entire dryer.", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 38.543, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.45, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "44", "pred": "Note: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "raw_output": "To get plastic out of a clothes dryer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Use a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper to carefully scrape the softened plastic off, avoiding damage to the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 teaspoons of nail polish remover to a clean cloth and rub the affected area to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Wipe the area with a damp cloth to rinse off the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 teaspoon of liquid laundry soap with 1 cup of water, dip a clean cloth in the solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse thoroughly with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nAlternatively, if the plastic is stubborn:\n- Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic, then attempt to peel it off.\n- If the plastic cannot be fully removed, consider replacing the dryer drum or the entire dryer, and consult a service technician for this repair.\n\nNote: Modern dryers have a synthetic enamel coating that resists heat and wear, so minor plastic residue is unlikely to cause serious damage.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to remove plastic from a clothes dryer:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper, taking care not to damage the dryer's interior.\n3. Apply 1 to 2 tsp. nail polish remover to a clean cloth, and rub the plastic to dissolve it.\n4. Rub the area with a damp cloth to rinse the nail polish remover.\n5. Mix 1 tsp. liquid laundry soap with 1 cup water. Dip a clean cloth in this solution, and rub the area again.\n6. Rinse well with a cloth dipped in clean water.\n\nIf the plastic does not come off with this method, another option is to use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic until it becomes soft and pliable. Then, attempt to peel it off carefully. However, be aware that this method may not work, and in that case, replacing the drum or the entire dryer may be necessary. It is recommended to call a service technician for this type of repair.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question of how to get plastic out of clothes dryer is:\n\n1. Run the dryer on a regular setting for 20 minutes to soften the plastic.\n2. Scrape the plastic off with a wooden or plastic spatula or paint scraper.\n3. Apply nail polish remover to dissolve the plastic.\n4. Rinse with a damp cloth and soap solution.\n5. Use a hair dryer or heat gun to heat the plastic if necessary.\n\nIf the plastic still does not come off, consider replacing the drum or the entire dryer.", "question": "how to get plastic out of clothes dryer", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 38.497, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.94, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "46", "pred": "These steps are part of preparing the quilt for mitered binding, as described in the passage.", "raw_output": "To fold a quilt, follow these steps:\n\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.\n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, making sure the fold is on the bias.\n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n5. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, again making sure the fold is on the bias.\n\nThese steps are part of preparing the quilt for mitered binding, as described in the passage.", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "how to fold a quilt", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 23.71, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "47", "pred": "These steps are part of preparing the quilt for mitered binding, as described in the passage.", "raw_output": "To fold a quilt, follow these steps:\n\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.\n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, making sure the fold is on the bias.\n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n5. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, again making sure the fold is on the bias.\n\nThese steps are part of preparing the quilt for mitered binding, as described in the passage.", "gt": "To fold a quilt, follow these steps:\n\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.\n2. Fold the lower right corner up towards the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n3. Fold the lower left corner up towards the center of the quilt, again on the bias.\n4. Fold the top left corner down towards the center of the quilt, again on the bias.\n5. Finally, fold the top right corner down towards the center of the quilt, making sure the fold is also on the bias.", "question": "how to fold a quilt", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 23.831, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.88, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "48", "pred": "These steps are part of preparing the quilt for mitered binding, as described in the passage.", "raw_output": "To fold a quilt, follow these steps:\n\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface such as a bed or table.\n2. Fold the lower right corner up toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n3. Fold the lower left corner up toward the center of the quilt, making sure the fold is on the bias.\n4. Fold the top left corner down toward the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n5. Fold the top right corner down toward the center of the quilt, again making sure the fold is on the bias.\n\nThese steps are part of preparing the quilt for mitered binding, as described in the passage.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to fold a quilt:\n\n1. Lay the quilt on a flat surface.\n2. Fold the lower right corner up towards the center of the quilt, ensuring the fold is on the bias.\n3. Fold the lower left corner up towards the center of the quilt, also on the bias.\n4. Fold the top left corner down towards the center of the quilt, again on the bias.\n5. Fold the top right corner down towards the center of the quilt, again on the bias.\n\nThese steps will result in a neatly folded quilt with four even corners. Note that the size of the quilt is not specified in the passages, so these steps can be applied to quilts of any size.", "question": "how to fold a quilt", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 23.716, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 4.6, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
