{"id": "123", "pred": "The name Teresa has multiple meanings across different cultures and languages. In Greek, the meaning of Teresa is \"Reap\" or \"Harvester,\" derived from \"therizein,\" meaning \"to harvest.\" It is also associated with the Greek island Therasia. In Italian, the name means \"Harvester,\" while in Spanish, it is linked to the names of two popular saints, Teresa of Avila and Thérèse of Lisieux. In American usage, the meaning is \"Reap\" or \"from Therasia.\" Additionally, the name Teresa is considered the Spanish, Portuguese, and Italian form of Theresa, which in Greek means \"Reaper.\" The name's usage as a female given name dates back to pre-Middle Ages in Spain and Portugal.", "raw_output": "The name Teresa has multiple meanings across different cultures and languages. In Greek, the meaning of Teresa is \"Reap\" or \"Harvester,\" derived from \"therizein,\" meaning \"to harvest.\" It is also associated with the Greek island Therasia. In Italian, the name means \"Harvester,\" while in Spanish, it is linked to the names of two popular saints, Teresa of Avila and Thérèse of Lisieux. In American usage, the meaning is \"Reap\" or \"from Therasia.\" Additionally, the name Teresa is considered the Spanish, Portuguese, and Italian form of Theresa, which in Greek means \"Reaper.\" The name's usage as a female given name dates back to pre-Middle Ages in Spain and Portugal.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the meaning of the name Teresa is:\n\n* Reap; from Therasia (Greek)\n* Reaper; from Therasia (American)\n* Harvester (Italian)\n* Name of two popular saints 'Teresa of Avila' and 'Therese of Lisieux' (Spanish)\n\nIt is also mentioned that the name Teresa is a Greek baby name, an American baby name, an Italian baby name, and a Spanish baby name.\n\nTherefore, the meaning of the name Teresa can vary depending on the culture and language, but it generally means \"reaper\" or \"harvester\", and is also associated with the names of two popular saints.", "question": "teresa name meaning", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.35, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.12, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "124", "pred": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "raw_output": "Answer: To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "gt": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4 x 4 skids to a length of 70 2/4\" and set and level them according to the shed building plans. Cut two 2 x 6 rim joists at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". Assemble the floor and place it on the skids as indicated in the floor framing section of the plans. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids with four joist clip angles. Cut two more 4 x 4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids and, when the frame is square, toenail the floor joists to the skids. Attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring onto the frame. The walls for the shed are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. The size of the shed can be adjusted as needed.", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.771, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 76.84, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "125", "pred": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "raw_output": "Answer: To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the process of building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site with compacted gravel, cutting and setting the 4 x 4 skids, cutting and assembling the floor with 2 x 6 rim joists and joists, anchoring the frame to the skids, attaching tongue-and-groove plywood flooring, constructing the walls separately, and raising them onto the frame.", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.832, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.5, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "126", "pred": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "raw_output": "Answer: To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "gt": "Building a lean-to shed involves preparing the site, cutting and assembling the skids, flooring, and walls. To prepare the site, a 4\" layer of compacted gravel is recommended. The skids are cut to 70 2/4\" and set and leveled according to the shed building plans. Two 2 x 6 rim joists are cut at 7 3/4\" and six joists at 44 3/4\". The floor is assembled and set on the skids, checked for square, and anchored to the skids with four joist clip angles. The walls are constructed separately and then raised onto the frame. Pressure-treated 4 by 4s are used for the skids, while 2 by 4s are used for the rim joists and flooring. 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood is attached to the frame for the flooring.", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.914, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.14, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "127", "pred": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "raw_output": "Answer: To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "gt": "Sure! Here's my attempt to answer your question based on the provided passages:\n\nHow do you prepare the site for building a lean-to shed?\n\nAccording to passage 1, step 1, you should prepare the site by laying down a 4\" layer of compacted gravel. This will provide a stable base for the shed's foundation. Additionally, passage 2 mentions cutting two pressure-treated 4x4s to a length of 6 feet for the skids, which will serve as the foundation for the shed. Finally, passage 3 provides instructions for building the shed's floor, including cutting rim joists to a length of 6 feet. Based on these passages, it appears that preparing the site involves laying down a layer of gravel and setting the skids in place before constructing the floor.", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.75, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 27.47, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "129", "pred": "To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "raw_output": "Answer: To build a lean-to shed, start by preparing the site with a 4-inch layer of compacted gravel. Cut two 4x4 skids to 70 2/4 inches and set them level according to the shed plans. Cut two 2x6 rim joists to 7 3/4 inches and six joists to 44 3/4 inches, then assemble the floor frame and place it on the skids. Check for squareness and anchor the frame to the skids using four joist clip angles. Alternatively, use pressure-treated 4x4s cut to 6 feet for the skids, position the floor frame on them, toenail the joists to the skids, and attach 3/4-inch tongue-and-groove plywood flooring. The walls are built separately and then raised onto the floor frame. The shed dimensions can be adjusted in increments of 2 or 4 feet, with a standard size being 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "gt": "What are the dimensions of the shed in passage 3?\n\nThe dimensions of the shed in passage 3 are 6 feet long by 4 feet wide.", "question": "building a lean-to shed", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.89, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 16.36, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "130", "pred": "These points illustrate differences in", "raw_output": "The passage highlights several differences between Vietnam and India:\n\n1. Education Trends: The trends in India are described as \"much more typical\" compared to Vietnam. Vietnam's education system is relatively equitable, meaning poorer children receive the same educational opportunities as wealthier children, reducing their disadvantage. In contrast, India's early focus was on getting children into school, emphasizing resources, facilities, school setup, and teacher training.\n\n2. Historical Context: India gained independence from British colonial rule in 1947, while Vietnam gained independence from French colonial rule in 1945. The United Kingdom lost India, and France lost its colony of Indochina (modern-day Vietnam) around the same time after World War II.\n\n3. Post-Colonial Outcomes: After British withdrawal, India experienced division along religious lines (Hindus, Muslims, and Sikhs), leading to conflicts. The passage does not mention similar post-colonial divisions in Vietnam.\n\n4. Bilateral Relations: India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. India and Vietnam have strong bilateral relations, with cultural and economic ties dating back to the 2nd century, including influence from the Indic Chăm Pa kingdom on Vietnamese music.\n\nThese points illustrate differences in", "gt": "The passages suggest that trends in India are more typical than those in Vietnam, particularly in the area of education. Vietnam's education system is described as relatively equitable, where poorer children receive the same treatment as their better-off counterparts. In contrast, India's early focus was on getting children into school, which involved setting up schools, and training teachers. Politically, India supported Vietnam's independence from France and opposed American involvement in the Vietnam War. Both countries have a history of colonialism, with India being a former colony of the United Kingdom and Vietnam of France. Post-colonization, India experienced division and conflict among Hindus, Muslims and Sikhs. Cultural and economic links between India and Vietnam date back to the 2nd century.", "question": "difference between vietnam and india", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.758, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "132", "pred": "Answer:", "raw_output": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can be used to prevent the garlic bulbs from rolling.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, and use your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the oven until the cloves are soft, which may take up to 45 minutes to an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\n\nAfter roasting, store the cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days. You can also season the cloves with herbs and spices like cayenne, chipotle pepper powder, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, oregano, thyme, rosemary, tarragon, or basil to complement your dish.\n\nAnswer:", "gt": "Preheat your oven to 400°F (205° C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves to expose them. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices if you like. Roasting time is dependent on the size of the cloves and may take up to 45 minutes or an hour for larger cloves. Once roasted, the garlic cloves can be stored in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.591, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "133", "pred": "Answer:", "raw_output": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can be used to prevent the garlic bulbs from rolling.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, and use your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the oven until the cloves are soft, which may take up to 45 minutes to an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\n\nAfter roasting, store the cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days. You can also season the cloves with herbs and spices like cayenne, chipotle pepper powder, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, oregano, thyme, rosemary, tarragon, or basil to complement your dish.\n\nAnswer:", "gt": "To roast individual garlic cloves, preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C). Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the garlic bulb, leaving the skins of the cloves intact. Cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves to expose them. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up, and drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, rubbing it over the cloves. Roast the cloves in the preheated oven for about 45 minutes to an hour, or until they are softened. Store the roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.691, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "135", "pred": "Answer:", "raw_output": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can be used to prevent the garlic bulbs from rolling.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, and use your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the oven until the cloves are soft, which may take up to 45 minutes to an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\n\nAfter roasting, store the cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days. You can also season the cloves with herbs and spices like cayenne, chipotle pepper powder, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, oregano, thyme, rosemary, tarragon, or basil to complement your dish.\n\nAnswer:", "gt": "Here are brief answers to the question on how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nPassage 1: The passage does not provide information on how to roast individual garlic cloves specifically. However, it does mention that larger garlic cloves may take up to an hour to soften when roasted, so it is possible to assume that smaller cloves will take less time.\n\nPassage 2: This passage provides some options for seasoning the garlic before roasting, but it does not provide instructions on how to roast individual garlic cloves.\n\nPassage 3: This passage provides a method for roasting entire garlic heads, but it does not address how to roast individual garlic cloves. The passage does mention that cutting the top of the garlic bulb and exposing the individual cloves can help them roast more evenly, but it does not provide specific instructions on how to do this.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.793, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 1.8, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "136", "pred": "Answer:", "raw_output": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can be used to prevent the garlic bulbs from rolling.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, and use your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the oven until the cloves are soft, which may take up to 45 minutes to an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\n\nAfter roasting, store the cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days. You can also season the cloves with herbs and spices like cayenne, chipotle pepper powder, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, oregano, thyme, rosemary, tarragon, or basil to complement your dish.\n\nAnswer:", "gt": "Sure! Here's how to roast individual garlic cloves based on the provided passages:\n\nTo roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of each clove, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices, if desired.\n7. Roast the garlic in the preheated oven for up to 45 minutes or until the cloves are softened to your liking.\n8. Store roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\n\nNote that the roasting time may vary depending on the size of your garlic cloves, so check on them periodically to avoid overcooking.", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.671, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "137", "pred": "Answer:", "raw_output": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, but keep the skins of the individual cloves intact.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut about 1/4 to 1/2 inch from the top of the cloves, exposing the individual garlic cloves.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. A muffin pan can be used to prevent the garlic bulbs from rolling.\n5. Drizzle a couple of teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, and use your fingers to rub the oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the oven until the cloves are soft, which may take up to 45 minutes to an hour depending on the size of the cloves.\n\nAfter roasting, store the cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days. You can also season the cloves with herbs and spices like cayenne, chipotle pepper powder, Cajun seasoning, curry powder, oregano, thyme, rosemary, tarragon, or basil to complement your dish.\n\nAnswer:", "gt": "To roast individual garlic cloves, follow these steps:\n\n1. Preheat your oven to 400°F (205°C).\n2. Peel and discard the papery outer layers of the whole garlic bulb, leaving intact the skins of the individual cloves of garlic.\n3. Using a sharp knife, cut 1/4 to a 1/2 inch from the top of cloves, exposing the individual cloves of garlic.\n4. Place the garlic heads in a baking pan, cut side up. (A muffin pan works great for this, as it keeps the garlic bulbs from rolling around.)\n5. Drizzle a couple teaspoons of olive oil over each exposed head, using your fingers to rub the olive oil over all the cut, exposed garlic cloves.\n6. Roast in the preheated oven for 45 minutes to an hour, or until the cloves are softened.\n7. Store roasted garlic cloves in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to 10 days.\n\nYou can also season the garlic cloves to taste with herbs and spices before roasting.", "question": "how to roast individual garlic cloves", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.684, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "138", "pred": "When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "raw_output": "When an asset is discarded under the Income Tax Act, the treatment is as follows:\n\n1. The scrap value of the discarded asset is reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per section 43(6).\n2. The block concept is applied, where all assets within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are clubbed together and treated as a single asset.\n3. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is calculated as the opening written down value (WDV) of the asset minus the scrap value.\n4. This depreciation can be claimed even if the discarded asset is no longer used for business or profession in that year or subsequent years.\n5. Any alterations to the value of the block must strictly follow the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\nAnswer: When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "gt": "Based on the Income Tax Act, when an asset is discarded but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded as per section 43(6). This is done under the block concept, where all assets of the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are considered as a single asset. Any changes to the block's value must abide by the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961. Additionally, the assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset, even if it's not used for the purposes of business or profession in that year and subsequent years.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.817, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.73, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "139", "pred": "When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "raw_output": "When an asset is discarded under the Income Tax Act, the treatment is as follows:\n\n1. The scrap value of the discarded asset is reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per section 43(6).\n2. The block concept is applied, where all assets within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are clubbed together and treated as a single asset.\n3. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is calculated as the opening written down value (WDV) of the asset minus the scrap value.\n4. This depreciation can be claimed even if the discarded asset is no longer used for business or profession in that year or subsequent years.\n5. Any alterations to the value of the block must strictly follow the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\nAnswer: When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, when an asset is discarded but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded. The block concept is followed, where assets of the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are grouped together and considered as a single asset. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, even if it is not used for business or profession in that year and subsequent years.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.933, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 35.96, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "140", "pred": "When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "raw_output": "When an asset is discarded under the Income Tax Act, the treatment is as follows:\n\n1. The scrap value of the discarded asset is reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per section 43(6).\n2. The block concept is applied, where all assets within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are clubbed together and treated as a single asset.\n3. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is calculated as the opening written down value (WDV) of the asset minus the scrap value.\n4. This depreciation can be claimed even if the discarded asset is no longer used for business or profession in that year or subsequent years.\n5. Any alterations to the value of the block must strictly follow the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\nAnswer: When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that when an asset is discarded by a business but not sold, the Income Tax Act allows the scrap value of the asset to be deducted from the opening work-in-progress (WDV) of such asset in the previous year in which it was discarded. This reduction is taken into account while computing the depreciation expense for the subsequent years. However, any changes made to the value of the block must be made strictly in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of Income Tax Act 1961.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 42.05, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 45.65, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "141", "pred": "When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "raw_output": "When an asset is discarded under the Income Tax Act, the treatment is as follows:\n\n1. The scrap value of the discarded asset is reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per section 43(6).\n2. The block concept is applied, where all assets within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are clubbed together and treated as a single asset.\n3. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is calculated as the opening written down value (WDV) of the asset minus the scrap value.\n4. This depreciation can be claimed even if the discarded asset is no longer used for business or profession in that year or subsequent years.\n5. Any alterations to the value of the block must strictly follow the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\nAnswer: When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nAccording to passage 1, when an asset is discarded by the business but not sold, the scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which such asset is discarded. This reduction is allowed under section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act.\n\nPassage 3 further states that the assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of such discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even though such discarded asset is not used for the purposes of business or profession in such year and subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment when an asset is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the block value of the asset by the scrap value in the previous year in which it is discarded, and to claim depreciation on the residual value of the asset in the year of discard and subsequent years.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.992, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.71, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "142", "pred": "When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "raw_output": "When an asset is discarded under the Income Tax Act, the treatment is as follows:\n\n1. The scrap value of the discarded asset is reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per section 43(6).\n2. The block concept is applied, where all assets within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation are clubbed together and treated as a single asset.\n3. The assessee is entitled to claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset, which is calculated as the opening written down value (WDV) of the asset minus the scrap value.\n4. This depreciation can be claimed even if the discarded asset is no longer used for business or profession in that year or subsequent years.\n5. Any alterations to the value of the block must strictly follow the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.\n\nAnswer: When an asset is discarded, its scrap value is deducted from the block, and depreciation is allowed on the residual value (opening WDV minus scrap value), even if the asset is no longer used for business, as per section 43(6) and the block concept under Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act, 1961.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the treatment for an asset that is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is as follows:\n\nThe scrap value of the asset can be reduced from the block in the previous year in which the asset is discarded, as per Section 43(6) of the Income Tax Act. The block concept applies to all assets falling within the same class and subject to the same rate of depreciation, and any alterations to the value of the block must be made in accordance with the provisions of Chapter IV D of the Income Tax Act. The assessee can claim depreciation on the residual value of the discarded asset (i.e., opening WDV of such asset less scrap value) even if the asset is not used for business or professional purposes in that year or subsequent years.\n\nTherefore, the treatment for an asset that is discarded as per the Income Tax Act is to reduce the scrap value from the block in the previous year and claim depreciation on the residual value.", "question": "what is the treatment when asset is discarded as per income tax act", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.864, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.58, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "144", "pred": "Additionally, when attaching the gusset to the pencil case body, align the ends with the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends.", "raw_output": "To sew a zipper gusset, follow these steps:\n\n1. Trace the pattern onto the fabric and cut it out.\n2. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper to secure it.\n3. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper, keeping a 1/4″ distance from the center, with right sides facing each other.\n4. Switch to a zipper foot presser foot and sew a line close to the seam line to secure the gusset in place.\n5. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of the main gusset with the right side facing down, then stitch along the seam line.\n6. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line.\n7. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset.\n8. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2″.\n9. Switch back to a normal presser foot and stitch along the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabrics together neatly.\n\nAdditionally, when attaching the gusset to the pencil case body, align the ends with the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends.", "gt": "Unable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "how to sew a zipper gusset", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 42.096, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "146", "pred": "Additionally, when attaching the gusset to the pencil case body, align the ends with the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends.", "raw_output": "To sew a zipper gusset, follow these steps:\n\n1. Trace the pattern onto the fabric and cut it out.\n2. Make a few stitches near the top stop of the zipper to secure it.\n3. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper, keeping a 1/4″ distance from the center, with right sides facing each other.\n4. Switch to a zipper foot presser foot and sew a line close to the seam line to secure the gusset in place.\n5. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of the main gusset with the right side facing down, then stitch along the seam line.\n6. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and topstitch near the seam line.\n7. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset.\n8. Ensure the width of the zipper between the gusset measures 1/2″.\n9. Switch back to a normal presser foot and stitch along the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabrics together neatly.\n\nAdditionally, when attaching the gusset to the pencil case body, align the ends with the center markings and sew from marking to marking at both ends.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to sew a zipper gusset:\n\n1. Make a casing in the side seams by stitching the outer edge of the gusset and the back side seam.\n2. Slide the boning right down into the casing.\n3. Push the seam allowance to the back and tack the casing to the lining, if necessary, so it doesn't move.\n4. Make a zipper sandwich by placing one piece of lining fabric face up, followed by the zipper, and then the outer fabric right side facing down.\n5. Sew across the top of the zipper sandwich with a zipper foot, making sure to line up the three edges.\n6. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper.\n7. Trace all pattern pieces to the remaining fabric and cut them out.\n8. Pin the zipper gusset to the zipper, with the right sides facing each other, and sew a line near the seam line to secure the position.\n9. Place the zipper gusset lining on top of the zipper gusset, with the right sides facing down, and stitch along the seam line.\n10. Flip the lining to the bottom of the main zipper gusset and top stitch near the seam line.\n11. Repeat the process for the other side of the zipper gusset.\n12. The width of the zipper between the gusset should measure 1/2\".\n13. Change the presser foot back to a normal foot and stitch along the seam allowance around the zipper gusset to attach the main and lining fabrics together nicely.\n\nTherefore, to sew a zipper gusset, you need to make a casing in the side seams, insert the boning, make a zipper sandwich, sew the zipper gusset to the zipper, and attach the lining to the zipper gusset.", "question": "how to sew a zipper gusset", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.963, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 1.94, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "150", "pred": "To make hard-boiled eggs, place the cold eggs in a single layer in a pan and cover them with cold water by about 1 inch. Place the pan on the stove over high heat. Once the water comes to a boil, turn off the heat, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat. Let the eggs sit in the hot water for about 9 minutes for medium eggs, 12 minutes for large eggs, and 15 minutes for extra-large eggs. After cooking, pour out the hot water and cool the eggs with cold water.", "raw_output": "Answer: To make hard-boiled eggs, place the cold eggs in a single layer in a pan and cover them with cold water by about 1 inch. Place the pan on the stove over high heat. Once the water comes to a boil, turn off the heat, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat. Let the eggs sit in the hot water for about 9 minutes for medium eggs, 12 minutes for large eggs, and 15 minutes for extra-large eggs. After cooking, pour out the hot water and cool the eggs with cold water.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to bake hard-boiled eggs:\n\n1. Place cold eggs in a single layer in a pan with enough cold water to cover them by about 1 inch.\n2. Place the pan on the stove over high heat.\n3. As soon as the water comes to a boil, turn the heat off, cover the pan, and remove it from the heat.\n4. Allow the eggs to sit in the water for the appropriate amount of time, depending on the size of the eggs (9 minutes for medium, 12 minutes for large, and 15 minutes for extra-large).\n\nNote: Do not peek during the cooking process, and do not worry if the eggs are not perfectly symmetrical or have a slightly greenish-gray yolk.", "question": "how to bake hard boiled egg", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 20.367, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 66.19, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "158", "pred": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, follow these steps:\n\n1. Slide the Notifications panel open, and then tap on \"Storage.\"\n2. Tap on \"Factory data reset.\"\n3. Tap on \"Reset phone,\" and then tap \"Erase everything.\"\n\nBefore proceeding, ensure that you back up any data and files you want to keep.", "raw_output": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, follow these steps:\n\n1. Slide the Notifications panel open, and then tap on \"Storage.\"\n2. Tap on \"Factory data reset.\"\n3. Tap on \"Reset phone,\" and then tap \"Erase everything.\"\n\nBefore proceeding, ensure that you back up any data and files you want to keep.", "gt": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, you can follow the steps mentioned in passage 3. Slide the Notifications panel open, tap on Storage, then Factory data reset, and finally tap on Reset phone and Erase everything.", "question": "how to factory reset a htc one vx", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 12.935, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 70.77, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "159", "pred": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, follow these steps:\n\n1. Slide the Notifications panel open, and then tap on \"Storage.\"\n2. Tap on \"Factory data reset.\"\n3. Tap on \"Reset phone,\" and then tap \"Erase everything.\"\n\nBefore proceeding, ensure that you back up any data and files you want to keep.", "raw_output": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, follow these steps:\n\n1. Slide the Notifications panel open, and then tap on \"Storage.\"\n2. Tap on \"Factory data reset.\"\n3. Tap on \"Reset phone,\" and then tap \"Erase everything.\"\n\nBefore proceeding, ensure that you back up any data and files you want to keep.", "gt": "To factory reset an HTC One VX, follow these steps:\n\n1. Switch off the device by holding down the Power button for a while.\n2. Press and hold the Volume Down + Power key for a couple of seconds until Bootloader Mode menu appears on the screen.\n3. From the menu, choose \"Factory Data Reset\" by using Volume buttons to scroll and Power key to select.\n4. Confirm the reset by pressing \"Erase Everything\".\n5. Release all keys when the process is complete.", "question": "how to factory reset a htc one vx", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 12.879, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.68, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "162", "pred": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen, a protein produced by the prostate gland, measured in a man’s blood. The results are typically reported in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL). Elevated PSA levels may indicate various conditions, including prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate (prostatitis). For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/mL and 10.0 ng/mL may suggest a 25% chance of prostate cancer, according to the American Cancer Society (ACS). A free PSA test may also be used to assess the ratio of free to total PSA to help differentiate between cancer and non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "raw_output": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen, a protein produced by the prostate gland, measured in a man’s blood. The results are typically reported in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL). Elevated PSA levels may indicate various conditions, including prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate (prostatitis). For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/mL and 10.0 ng/mL may suggest a 25% chance of prostate cancer, according to the American Cancer Society (ACS). A free PSA test may also be used to assess the ratio of free to total PSA to help differentiate between cancer and non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "gt": "PSA, or Prostate-specific antigen, is a protein produced by cells of the prostate gland. The level of PSA in a man's blood is measured by the PSA test, which involves sending a blood sample to a laboratory for analysis. The results are usually reported as nanograms of PSA per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate conditions such as prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition like prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml may indicate prostate cancer, benign prostate hyperplasia, or inflammation of the prostate.", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 25.182, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.32, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "166", "pred": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen, a protein produced by the prostate gland, measured in a man’s blood. The results are typically reported in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL). Elevated PSA levels may indicate various conditions, including prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate (prostatitis). For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/mL and 10.0 ng/mL may suggest a 25% chance of prostate cancer, according to the American Cancer Society (ACS). A free PSA test may also be used to assess the ratio of free to total PSA to help differentiate between cancer and non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "raw_output": "PSA levels refer to the concentration of prostate-specific antigen, a protein produced by the prostate gland, measured in a man’s blood. The results are typically reported in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL). Elevated PSA levels may indicate various conditions, including prostate cancer, benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), or inflammation of the prostate (prostatitis). For example, total PSA levels between 4.0 ng/mL and 10.0 ng/mL may suggest a 25% chance of prostate cancer, according to the American Cancer Society (ACS). A free PSA test may also be used to assess the ratio of free to total PSA to help differentiate between cancer and non-cancer causes of elevated PSA.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, PSA levels refer to the amount of prostate-specific antigen present in a man's blood. The level of PSA is measured in nanograms per milliliter (ng/mL) of blood. Elevated PSA levels may indicate prostate cancer, a noncancerous condition such as prostatitis, or an enlarged prostate. Normal PSA levels range between 4.0 ng/ml and 10.0 ng/ml, and a moderate elevation in PSA levels may require further testing, such as a free PSA test, to determine the ratio of free to total PSA and help distinguish between prostate cancer and other non-cancer causes of elevated PSA. Therefore, the PSA test is used to monitor the progression of prostate cancer and to detect elevated PSA levels that may indicate prostate cancer or other prostate issues.", "question": "what is psa levels mean", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 25.242, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 58.9, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "167", "pred": "7", "raw_output": "To make a graph for your research, follow these steps based on the provided passage:\n\n1. **Collect your data**: Gather all the information you want to represent. For example, if you're comparing rainfall amounts across different months, collect the data for each month.\n\n2. **Choose the right type of graph**: Decide whether a bar graph or a line graph is more suitable. Bar graphs are ideal for comparing different categories or groups, such as rainfall in different months or average salaries in different states.\n\n3. **Title your graph**: Give your graph a clear and descriptive title that reflects what it represents.\n\n4. **Set up the axes**: \n   - The x-axis (horizontal axis) typically shows the categories you are comparing (e.g., months of the year).\n   - The y-axis (vertical axis) shows the scale of the data (e.g., inches of rainfall). Make sure the scale is appropriate and proportional to your data.\n\n5. **Draw the bars**: For a bar graph, draw rectangular bars for each category. The height of each bar should be proportional to the value it represents.\n\n6. **Add color and clarity**: Use colors to make your graph visually appealing and easier to understand. Ensure that the graph clearly shows the comparisons you intended.\n\n7", "gt": "To create a graph for your research, you first need to collect all of your data, remembering that the graph should show a comparison among categories. If you are creating a bar graph, you will use rectangular bars to represent different values. You need to title your graph and determine how high your graph needs to go. If your data points are substantially different in scale, you can use a pie chart. The y-axis of your graph will have a scale, and each bar’s height should be proportionate to the size of the category it represents. Make sure to consider any disproportionate responses that might impact the scale of your graph. Regardless of the type of graph you choose, it should provide a clear visual representation of your information.", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.729, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "169", "pred": "7", "raw_output": "To make a graph for your research, follow these steps based on the provided passage:\n\n1. **Collect your data**: Gather all the information you want to represent. For example, if you're comparing rainfall amounts across different months, collect the data for each month.\n\n2. **Choose the right type of graph**: Decide whether a bar graph or a line graph is more suitable. Bar graphs are ideal for comparing different categories or groups, such as rainfall in different months or average salaries in different states.\n\n3. **Title your graph**: Give your graph a clear and descriptive title that reflects what it represents.\n\n4. **Set up the axes**: \n   - The x-axis (horizontal axis) typically shows the categories you are comparing (e.g., months of the year).\n   - The y-axis (vertical axis) shows the scale of the data (e.g., inches of rainfall). Make sure the scale is appropriate and proportional to your data.\n\n5. **Draw the bars**: For a bar graph, draw rectangular bars for each category. The height of each bar should be proportional to the value it represents.\n\n6. **Add color and clarity**: Use colors to make your graph visually appealing and easier to understand. Ensure that the graph clearly shows the comparisons you intended.\n\n7", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to create a bar graph for your research:\n\n1. Collect your data: The first step is to gather all the data you want to represent in your graph. Make sure the data is organized and easy to compare.\n2. Decide on the x-axis and y-axis: The x-axis typically represents the category or group being compared, while the y-axis represents the value or amount of each category.\n3. Determine the height of the graph: The height of the graph will depend on the highest value in your data set. Make sure to choose a height that allows for clear representation of all the data points.\n4. Create the bars: Use rectangular bars to represent each category, making sure the height of each bar is proportional to the value it represents.\n5. Add labels and titles: Label each bar with its corresponding category and add a title to the graph that describes the data being represented.\n6. Consider using a pie chart: If you have only a few data points to display and each one is substantially different in scale, a pie chart might be a better option.\n7. Format and refine: Make sure the graph is visually appealing and easy to read. Adjust the scaling of the y-axis if needed, and consider adding colors or shading to make the graph more engaging.\n\nBy following these steps, you should be able to create a clear and effective bar graph for your research. Good luck!", "question": "show me how can i make gragh for my research", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.956, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 1.16, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "170", "pred": "Always store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "raw_output": "To soften sugar, the method depends on the type of sugar:\n\n1. For **brown sugar**: Place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil, cover tightly, and leave it for a couple of days. Remove the damp paper towel afterward.\n\n2. For **granulated sugar**: Preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150–200°F). Place the hardened sugar in an ovenproof container and heat it for about 15 minutes. Tap it with a spoon—if it starts to break apart, turn off the oven and let it sit inside for an hour or two to dry completely.\n\n3. For **confectioners sugar**: Store it in a cool, dry place (not the refrigerator). If it becomes moist and lumpy, it may need to be sifted or dried similarly to granulated sugar.\n\nAlways store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "gt": "To soften brown sugar, you can place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Then put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil and cover tightly. Remove the damp paper towel in a couple of days. To make hardened granulated sugar soft again, preheat an oven to its lowest temperature, 150-200 degrees. Remove the sugar from its package and put it in an ovenproof container. Place it in the warm oven for approximately 15 minutes. Tap the sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn the oven off and leave the sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container.", "question": "how to soften sugar", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 35.011, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.79, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "172", "pred": "Always store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "raw_output": "To soften sugar, the method depends on the type of sugar:\n\n1. For **brown sugar**: Place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil, cover tightly, and leave it for a couple of days. Remove the damp paper towel afterward.\n\n2. For **granulated sugar**: Preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150–200°F). Place the hardened sugar in an ovenproof container and heat it for about 15 minutes. Tap it with a spoon—if it starts to break apart, turn off the oven and let it sit inside for an hour or two to dry completely.\n\n3. For **confectioners sugar**: Store it in a cool, dry place (not the refrigerator). If it becomes moist and lumpy, it may need to be sifted or dried similarly to granulated sugar.\n\nAlways store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "gt": "To soften sugar, place brown sugar in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil. Cover tightly. Remove damp paper towel in a couple of days. Alternatively, preheat oven to lowest temperature (150-200 degrees) and remove sugar from package. Put in an ovenproof container and place in the warm oven for 15 minutes. Tap sugar with a spoon and if it starts to fall apart, turn oven off and leave sugar in the oven for an hour or two to completely dry out. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container and confectioners sugar in a cool, dry location.", "question": "how to soften sugar", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 35.188, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.98, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "173", "pred": "Always store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "raw_output": "To soften sugar, the method depends on the type of sugar:\n\n1. For **brown sugar**: Place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil, cover tightly, and leave it for a couple of days. Remove the damp paper towel afterward.\n\n2. For **granulated sugar**: Preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150–200°F). Place the hardened sugar in an ovenproof container and heat it for about 15 minutes. Tap it with a spoon—if it starts to break apart, turn off the oven and let it sit inside for an hour or two to dry completely.\n\n3. For **confectioners sugar**: Store it in a cool, dry place (not the refrigerator). If it becomes moist and lumpy, it may need to be sifted or dried similarly to granulated sugar.\n\nAlways store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some ways to soften sugar:\n\n1. To soften brown sugar, place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar, and cover tightly. Remove the damp paper towel after a couple of days. (Passage 1)\n2. To soften hardened granulated sugar, preheat the oven to a low temperature (150-200°F), remove the sugar from its package, and place it in an ovenproof container. Warm the sugar in the oven for about 15 minutes, then tap it with a spoon. If it starts to fall apart, leave it in the oven for a longer period of time to completely dry out. Always store granulated sugar in an airtight container. (Passage 2)\n3. To keep brown sugar soft, pack it into the smallest container possible to minimize air exposure. (Passage 3)\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide information on how to soften other types of sugar, such as confectioner's sugar.", "question": "how to soften sugar", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 35.092, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.33, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "175", "pred": "Always store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "raw_output": "To soften sugar, the method depends on the type of sugar:\n\n1. For **brown sugar**: Place it in a plastic container with a small piece of plastic wrap or foil over the sugar. Put a damp paper towel or napkin on top of the plastic/foil, cover tightly, and leave it for a couple of days. Remove the damp paper towel afterward.\n\n2. For **granulated sugar**: Preheat the oven to the lowest temperature (150–200°F). Place the hardened sugar in an ovenproof container and heat it for about 15 minutes. Tap it with a spoon—if it starts to break apart, turn off the oven and let it sit inside for an hour or two to dry completely.\n\n3. For **confectioners sugar**: Store it in a cool, dry place (not the refrigerator). If it becomes moist and lumpy, it may need to be sifted or dried similarly to granulated sugar.\n\nAlways store all types of sugar in airtight containers to prevent hardening or moisture absorption.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to soften sugar:\n\n1. Place brown sugar in an airtight container with a damp paper towel or napkin on top. Cover tightly and let it sit for a couple of days. Remove the damp paper towel, and the sugar should be soft. (Passage 1)\n2. Preheat the oven to a low temperature (150-200 degrees), place hardened granulated sugar in an ovenproof container, and tap it with a spoon after 15 minutes. If it starts to fall apart, turn off the oven and leave it in for an hour or two to dry out completely. Store granulated sugar in an airtight container. (Passage 2)\n3. Keep brown sugar soft by storing it in an airtight container with minimal air space between the sugar and the lid. Pack the sugar into the smallest container possible. (Passage 3)", "question": "how to soften sugar", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 35.197, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.77, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "179", "pred": "This will allow your computer to automatically go to sleep after the specified period of inactivity.", "raw_output": "To change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Open the Power Options:\n   - For Windows 7: Click the Start Menu, enter \"Power Management Settings\" in the search field, and click \"Change Power Management settings.\"\n   - For Windows 10: In the search box on the taskbar, type \"power options\" and select Power Options.\n\n2. In the Power Options window, click on \"Change when the computer sleeps\" on the left side.\n\n3. Adjust the \"Put the computer to sleep\" setting to your desired idle time before the computer enters Sleep mode.\n\nThis will allow your computer to automatically go to sleep after the specified period of inactivity.", "gt": "Sure, here's a brief answer to the question \"How to change the sleep time on my computer\" based on the given passages:\n\nTo change the sleep time on your computer, follow these steps:\n\n1. Click the Start menu and select Control Panel.\n2. In the Control Panel, click on Power Options.\n3. In the Power Options window, click on \"Change when the computer sleeps\" on the left side of the window.\n4. In the \"Put the computer to sleep\" section, change the duration of time to the desired amount of time you want the computer to sit idle before it goes to sleep.\n5. Alternatively, you can also toggle off the option to enter Sleep mode automatically.\n\nIf you are using Windows 10, you can also search for \"Power Options\" in the taskbar search box and select it to open the Power Options window.\n\nIf you are using Windows 8.1 / Windows RT 8.1, you can move your mouse to the lower left-hand corner of the screen, right-click the Start button, and select Shut down or sign out, and then select Shut down.", "question": "how to change the sleep time on my computer", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 23.906, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.02, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "180", "pred": "The psychological effects of hugging include reducing existential fears, such as worry about mortality, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science. Even hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear can soothe these fears. Additionally, empathic listening and emotional intimacy fostered through relationships can create a \"psychological hug,\" making individuals feel emotionally supported and cared for.", "raw_output": "The psychological effects of hugging include reducing existential fears, such as worry about mortality, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science. Even hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear can soothe these fears. Additionally, empathic listening and emotional intimacy fostered through relationships can create a \"psychological hug,\" making individuals feel emotionally supported and cared for.", "gt": "Hugging has significant psychological effects. It creates a warm feeling that affects the entire body, akin to the effect of many drugs operating simultaneously. This could be due to the feeling of a loving embrace, leading to good relationships and emotional intimacy. It also enhances empathy and listening skills, which can lead to a psychological hug. Furthermore, research has found that hugging can significantly reduce worries about mortality and boost self-esteem. This soothing effect can be achieved even when hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear.", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.756, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.54, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "181", "pred": "The psychological effects of hugging include reducing existential fears, such as worry about mortality, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science. Even hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear can soothe these fears. Additionally, empathic listening and emotional intimacy fostered through relationships can create a \"psychological hug,\" making individuals feel emotionally supported and cared for.", "raw_output": "The psychological effects of hugging include reducing existential fears, such as worry about mortality, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science. Even hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear can soothe these fears. Additionally, empathic listening and emotional intimacy fostered through relationships can create a \"psychological hug,\" making individuals feel emotionally supported and cared for.", "gt": "Hugging has physiological benefits and can have a similar effect on the body as certain drugs. It can also lead to emotional intimacy and happy relationships. Hugging and touch, even with inanimate objects, can help reduce existential fears and worry about mortality.", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.561, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.11, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "182", "pred": "The psychological effects of hugging include reducing existential fears, such as worry about mortality, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science. Even hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear can soothe these fears. Additionally, empathic listening and emotional intimacy fostered through relationships can create a \"psychological hug,\" making individuals feel emotionally supported and cared for.", "raw_output": "The psychological effects of hugging include reducing existential fears, such as worry about mortality, as shown in a study published in the journal Psychological Science. Even hugging an inanimate object like a teddy bear can soothe these fears. Additionally, empathic listening and emotional intimacy fostered through relationships can create a \"psychological hug,\" making individuals feel emotionally supported and cared for.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, we can see that the physiological effects of hugging are numerous and significant. Hugs affect the entire body and can potentially be compared to the effects of multiple drugs operating at once. Furthermore, the act of hugging can also have psychological effects. Empathic listening can lead to emotional intimacy and happy relationships, as well as making conversation partners feel like they are receiving a hug, which can provide comfort and ease their existential fears. Overall, the psychological effects of hugging are positive and suggest that this simple gesture can have a significant impact on our emotional and physical wellbeing.", "question": "psychological effects of hugging", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.67, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 31.58, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "183", "pred": "Optional: While the roast is resting, prepare au jus by placing the roasting pan on the stove over high heat, adding wine, scraping brown bits, then adding beef stock and reducing the mixture by half. Season with salt and pepper to taste.", "raw_output": "To cook a small prime rib roast in the oven, follow these steps:\n\n1. Allow the roast to stand at room temperature for at least 1 hour before cooking.\n2. Preheat the oven to 450°F and let it preheat for a minimum of 15 minutes.\n3. Season the roast with only a small amount of salt and lots of fresh ground black pepper. Avoid using any other spices to preserve the prime rib's natural flavor.\n4. Place the roast on a rack in a roasting pan with the rib side down and the fatty side up.\n5. Roast at 450°F for approximately 15–20 minutes per pound, depending on the desired doneness (e.g., 15 minutes per pound for medium-rare).\n6. After roasting, remove the roast from the oven and let it rest for at least 15–20 minutes before carving.\n\nOptional: While the roast is resting, prepare au jus by placing the roasting pan on the stove over high heat, adding wine, scraping brown bits, then adding beef stock and reducing the mixture by half. Season with salt and pepper to taste.", "gt": "Before cooking the prime rib roast, let it stand at room temperature for at least 1 hour. Preheat the oven to 375 degrees F. Season the roast with a small amount of salt and lots of fresh ground black pepper. Place the roast on a rack in a pan with the rib side down and the fatty side up. Roast it in the oven for 1 hour. After cooking, you can prepare au jus by placing the roasting pan on the stove over high heat. Add wine and cook for 8-10 minutes, stirring and scraping any brown bits. Add beef stock and cook to reduce the juice by half, which might take about 20 minutes. Add salt and pepper to taste.", "question": "how to cook a small prime rib roast in oven", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 39.646, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.74, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "185", "pred": "It takes 6–8 hours on low in a crock pot to make chicken tender. Alternatively, you can cook it on high for 1 hour and then on low for about 5 hours, or 4 hours on high if you're short on time. The key is ensuring there is enough moisture to keep the chicken tender.", "raw_output": "Answer: It takes 6–8 hours on low in a crock pot to make chicken tender. Alternatively, you can cook it on high for 1 hour and then on low for about 5 hours, or 4 hours on high if you're short on time. The key is ensuring there is enough moisture to keep the chicken tender.", "gt": "To make chicken tender in a crock pot, you can cook it on low for 6 - 8 hours or until done. Alternatively, you can cook it on high for 1 hour, then on low for about 5 hours or until done. Another option is cooking it for 4 hours on high.", "question": "how long does it take to make chicken tender in crock pot", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.162, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 62.5, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "189", "pred": "It takes 6–8 hours on low in a crock pot to make chicken tender. Alternatively, you can cook it on high for 1 hour and then on low for about 5 hours, or 4 hours on high if you're short on time. The key is ensuring there is enough moisture to keep the chicken tender.", "raw_output": "Answer: It takes 6–8 hours on low in a crock pot to make chicken tender. Alternatively, you can cook it on high for 1 hour and then on low for about 5 hours, or 4 hours on high if you're short on time. The key is ensuring there is enough moisture to keep the chicken tender.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the answer to the question \"How long does it take to make chicken tender in a crock pot?\" is:\n\n6-8 hours on low heat, or 4 hours on high heat if you're short on time.\n\nThe first passage suggests cooking the chicken on low for 6-8 hours, or 1 hour on high and then 5 hours on low. The second passage repeats the same information. The third passage provides additional information that chicken breast will always come out tender as long as there is enough moisture, and suggests cooking it for 4 hours on high if you're short on time.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is 6-8 hours on low heat, or 4 hours on high heat if you're short on time.", "question": "how long does it take to make chicken tender in crock pot", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.104, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.71, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "191", "pred": "This method ensures the grouped pictures stand securely on a table top.", "raw_output": "To attach multiple picture frames together, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding. For joining two frames, use identical frames. For three frames, choose either three identical ones or one larger frame to serve as the middle frame.\n\n2. Lay the frames beside each other to assess the visual effect.\n\n3. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement. Ensure the hinge placement is at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to maintain stability when standing on a tabletop.\n\n4. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\n\n5. Repeat the process for additional frames if creating a group of three.\n\nThis method ensures the grouped pictures stand securely on a table top.", "gt": "To attach multiple picture frames together, you can use small hinges to connect the frames. Lay the frames beside each other to determine the desired arrangement. For two frames, use identical ones, and for three frames, either choose three identical frames or use a larger one as the middle frame. Mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides of the frames, ensuring it is the same distance from the bottom edges. Attach the hinges to securely join the frames.", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 25.193, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.23, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "193", "pred": "This method ensures the grouped pictures stand securely on a table top.", "raw_output": "To attach multiple picture frames together, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding. For joining two frames, use identical frames. For three frames, choose either three identical ones or one larger frame to serve as the middle frame.\n\n2. Lay the frames beside each other to assess the visual effect.\n\n3. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement. Ensure the hinge placement is at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to maintain stability when standing on a tabletop.\n\n4. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\n\n5. Repeat the process for additional frames if creating a group of three.\n\nThis method ensures the grouped pictures stand securely on a table top.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some steps for attaching multiple picture frames together:\n\nPassage 1: Table-Stand Grouping\nTo join two or three frames together, you can use identical frames or one larger frame as the middle piece. Place the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect, and then mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides. Attach two small hinges to the marked areas to join the frames together.\n\nPassage 2: Hanging Multiple Pictures Together\nBefore attaching the frames together, test different arrangements of the group of multiple pictures on the floor. Once you have decided on the layout, screw the frames to the backer board, repeating the process for each frame. Attach a hanging wire to the back of the frames and hang them on the wall to enjoy the finished result.\n\nPassage 3: Attaching Frames Together\nTo attach two frames together, place them back to back and mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides. The placement should be the same distance from the bottom edge of each frame to ensure stability when the grouped pictures are placed on a table. Attach two small hinges to the marked areas to join the frames together.\n\nIn conclusion, the recommended method for attaching multiple picture frames together involves using identical or matching frames, marking the hinge placement on the connecting sides, and attaching small hinges to join the frames together. It's also important to test different arrangements of the group of pictures on the floor before attaching the frames together, and to ensure that the frames are securely attached to the backer board before hanging them on the wall.", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 25.275, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.18, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "194", "pred": "This method ensures the grouped pictures stand securely on a table top.", "raw_output": "To attach multiple picture frames together, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding. For joining two frames, use identical frames. For three frames, choose either three identical ones or one larger frame to serve as the middle frame.\n\n2. Lay the frames beside each other to assess the visual effect.\n\n3. Place two frames back to back and mark the hinge placement. Ensure the hinge placement is at the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame to maintain stability when standing on a tabletop.\n\n4. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames.\n\n5. Repeat the process for additional frames if creating a group of three.\n\nThis method ensures the grouped pictures stand securely on a table top.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here are the steps to attach multiple picture frames together:\n\n1. Select two or three frames of the same wood molding (Passage 1).\n2. Lay the frames beside each other to see if you like the effect (Passage 1).\n3. Mark the hinge placement on the connecting sides of the frames, making sure the placement is the same distance from the bottom edges of each frame (Passage 1).\n4. Attach two small hinges to the connecting sides of the frames (Passage 1).\n5. Test ideas by laying the group of multiple pictures out on the floor first (Passage 2).\n6. Screw the frame to the backer board, repeat with each frame, and attach a hanging wire to the back (Passage 3).\n\nIt's important to note that the passages do not provide specific information on how to attach multiple frames together other than the steps mentioned above. Therefore, if more detailed instructions are needed, it may be best to consult additional resources or seek advice from a professional framer.", "question": "how to attach multiple picture frames together", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", 