{"id": "244", "pred": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "raw_output": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "gt": "The FEHA (Fair Employment and Housing Act) and the ADA (Americans with Disabilities Act) have several differences. The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector that have four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Another important difference is the requirement of disability. The ADA requires a physical or mental impairment to substantially limit a major life activity, while the FEHA only requires that a mental and physical disability limit a major life activity, without the need for it to be a substantial limit.", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.199, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.45, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "245", "pred": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "raw_output": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "gt": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA affects more employees and doesn't distinguish between private and public employers like ADA does.", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.253, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 64.86, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "246", "pred": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "raw_output": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main differences between the Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA) and the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) are:\n\n1. Scope of application: The ADA applies to all employers in the private sector with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA affects more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers like the ADA does.\n2. Definition of disability: The FEHA requires that a disability must limit a major life activity, while the ADA requires that a physical or mental impairment substantially limits a major life activity.\n3. Number of employees: The ADA applies to employers with four to fifteen employees, while the FEHA has no such requirement.\n\nTherefore, the main difference between FEHA and ADA is the scope of application and the definition of disability.", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.209, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.58, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "248", "pred": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "raw_output": "One difference between FEHA and ADA is that the ADA applies to all private sector employers with four to fifteen employees, while FEHA covers more employees and does not distinguish between private and public employers. Additionally, under the ADA, a disability must substantially limit a major life activity, whereas FEHA only requires that a disability limits a major life activity, not necessarily substantially.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here's a brief answer to the question:\n\nThe main difference between FEHA and ADA is that FEHA applies to all employers in California, regardless of size or sector, while ADA applies to private employers with 4 to 15 employees and does not cover public employers. Additionally, FEHA has a broader definition of disability, requiring only that a mental or physical disability limit a major life activity, whereas ADA requires a substantial limitation.", "question": "difference between feha and ada", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.175, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 56.57, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "249", "pred": "1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate.\n2. Rinse the pork chops.\n3. Prepare a spice mixture by combining seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring.\n4. Sprinkle the spice mixture on both sides of the wet pork chops.\n5. Use your hands to massage the spice rub into the meat.\n6. Let the seasoned pork chops stand for 10 minutes.\n7. Grill the chops over indirect heat until they are no longer pink inside, approximately 12 minutes per side.", "raw_output": "To grill pork chops according to the provided passage:\n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate.\n2. Rinse the pork chops.\n3. Prepare a spice mixture by combining seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring.\n4. Sprinkle the spice mixture on both sides of the wet pork chops.\n5. Use your hands to massage the spice rub into the meat.\n6. Let the seasoned pork chops stand for 10 minutes.\n7. Grill the chops over indirect heat until they are no longer pink inside, approximately 12 minutes per side.", "gt": "To grill pork chops according to allrecipes.com, start by preheating an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate. In a bowl, mix together the seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring until thoroughly combined. Rinse the pork chops, and sprinkle the wet chops on both sides with the spice mixture. With your hands, massage the spice rub into the meat and allow to stand for 10 minutes. Finally, grill the chops over indirect heat until no longer pink inside, about 12 minutes per side.", "question": "how to grill pork chops, allrecipes.com", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.849, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 78.01, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "250", "pred": "1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate.\n2. Rinse the pork chops.\n3. Prepare a spice mixture by combining seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring.\n4. Sprinkle the spice mixture on both sides of the wet pork chops.\n5. Use your hands to massage the spice rub into the meat.\n6. Let the seasoned pork chops stand for 10 minutes.\n7. Grill the chops over indirect heat until they are no longer pink inside, approximately 12 minutes per side.", "raw_output": "To grill pork chops according to the provided passage:\n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate.\n2. Rinse the pork chops.\n3. Prepare a spice mixture by combining seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring.\n4. Sprinkle the spice mixture on both sides of the wet pork chops.\n5. Use your hands to massage the spice rub into the meat.\n6. Let the seasoned pork chops stand for 10 minutes.\n7. Grill the chops over indirect heat until they are no longer pink inside, approximately 12 minutes per side.", "gt": "To grill pork chops according to allrecipes.com, preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate. In a bowl, mix together seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring. Rinse the pork chops and sprinkle the wet chops on both sides with the spice mixture. Massage the spice rub into the meat and let it stand for 10 minutes. Grill the chops over indirect heat for about 12 minutes per side or until they are no longer pink inside.", "question": "how to grill pork chops, allrecipes.com", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.885, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 82.96, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "251", "pred": "1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate.\n2. Rinse the pork chops.\n3. Prepare a spice mixture by combining seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring.\n4. Sprinkle the spice mixture on both sides of the wet pork chops.\n5. Use your hands to massage the spice rub into the meat.\n6. Let the seasoned pork chops stand for 10 minutes.\n7. Grill the chops over indirect heat until they are no longer pink inside, approximately 12 minutes per side.", "raw_output": "To grill pork chops according to the provided passage:\n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat and lightly oil the grate.\n2. Rinse the pork chops.\n3. Prepare a spice mixture by combining seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring.\n4. Sprinkle the spice mixture on both sides of the wet pork chops.\n5. Use your hands to massage the spice rub into the meat.\n6. Let the seasoned pork chops stand for 10 minutes.\n7. Grill the chops over indirect heat until they are no longer pink inside, approximately 12 minutes per side.", "gt": "To grill pork chops according to the instructions provided in Allrecipes.com, follow these steps: \n\n1. Preheat an outdoor grill for medium heat. \n2. Lightly oil the grate. \n3. Mix together seasoned salt, black pepper, garlic powder, onion powder, paprika, Worcestershire sauce, and smoke flavoring until well combined. \n4. Rinse pork chops and sprinkle them with the spice mixture on both sides. \n5. Massage the spice rub into the meat and let it stand for 10 minutes. \n6. Grill the chops over indirect heat for about 12 minutes per side, or until they are no longer pink inside.", "question": "how to grill pork chops, allrecipes.com", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.833, "status": "success", "eval_metrics":