can occur even without a fall or injury.\n\nIn summary, severe osteoporosis is characterized by significant bone weakening, heightened risk of fractures, and more pronounced symptoms.", "question": "what is severe osteoporosis", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 24.841, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.48, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "283", "pred": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. It is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, because, if, since, until, when, and while. In contrast, an adjective clause modifies a noun or a pronoun and is introduced by a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, that) or a subordinate conjunction (when, where). While both are dependent clauses, their functions differ: adverb clauses provide information about how, when, where, why, or under what conditions something happens, whereas adjective clauses describe or identify the noun or pronoun they modify.", "raw_output": "An adverb clause is a dependent clause that modifies a verb, adjective, or another adverb, usually modifying the verb. It is introduced by subordinate conjunctions such as after, although, because, if, since, until, when, and while. In contrast, an adjective clause modifies a noun or a pronoun and is introduced by a relative pronoun (who, whose, whom, which, that) or a subordinate conjunction (when, where). While both are dependent clauses, their functions differ: adverb clauses provide information about how, when, where, why, or under what conditions something happens, whereas adjective clauses describe or identify the noun or pronoun they modify.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an adverb clause modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, while an adjective clause modifies a noun or pronoun.", "question": "difference between an adverb clause and an adjective clause", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 23.246, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 31.17, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "286", "pred": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "raw_output": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "gt": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, start by preheating the oven to 325-350 degrees Fahrenheit. If you don't have time to defrost the roast, put it in the oven at 250 F for about an hour. Afterwards, take it out and season it with salt, pepper, and any seasonings you prefer, such as thyme, oregano or garlic. Rub the surface with 2 tsp. of olive oil. Place the roast in a roasting pan with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards, and put it back in the oven for about 1.5 to 3 hours depending on its size, or about 20-30 minutes per pound. Make sure the roast is cooked thoroughly by using a meat thermometer; the internal temperature should reach 150 degrees Fahrenheit. Once it is cooked, cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.094, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "287", "pred": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "raw_output": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "gt": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 325 degrees F. Place the frozen roast in a roasting pan and rub the surface with olive oil and season with salt, pepper, and desired seasonings. Place the roast, uncovered, in the preheated oven. Cook for about 20-30 minutes per pound or until the internal temperature reaches 150 degrees F. Once cooked, cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.283, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.66, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "289", "pred": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "raw_output": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven:\n\nPassage 1 states to defrost the roast first and then place it in the oven at 250°F for about an hour. After taking it out, season the roast and turn the oven temperature to 350°F. Cook for 1.5 to 3 hours, depending on the size of the roast, making sure it's cooked all the way through.\n\nPassage 2 provides specific instructions for preparing the roast before cooking. Rub the surface of the frozen pork loin with olive oil and season with salt, pepper, and any desired herbs. Place the roast in a preheated oven at 325°F and determine the cooking time based on the weight of the roast.\n\nPassage 3 suggests starting by preheating the oven to 350°F and then placing the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on a roasting rack. Cook for 20-30 minutes per pound, inserting a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature once cooked. Once the temperature reaches 150°F, remove the roast from the oven and let it rest for 30 minutes before slicing and serving.\n\nBased on these passages, the general steps to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven are:\n\n1. Defrost the roast if not already thawed.\n2. Season the roast with salt, pepper, and herbs.\n3. Place the roast in a preheated oven at the appropriate temperature (250°F or 325°F) for the specified time.\n4. Check the internal temperature of the roast using a meat thermometer to ensure it reaches the desired doneness.\n5. Remove the roast from the oven and let it rest for 30 minutes before slicing and serving.", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.078, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 32.48, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "290", "pred": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "raw_output": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven:\n\n1. Defrost the pork loin if possible. If you don't have time to defrost, you can place it in the oven at 250°F for about an hour.\n2. Preheat the oven to 325°F (passage 2).\n3. Rub the surface of the frozen pork loin with 2 tsp of olive oil and season the outside with salt, pepper, and desired herbs (thyme, oregano, or garlic work well) (passage 2).\n4. Place the pork loin in a roasting pan and put it in the preheated oven (passage 2).\n5. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound, or until the internal temperature reaches 150°F (passage 3).\n6. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature of the meat (passage 3).\n7. Once the meat is cooked, remove it from the oven and cover it with aluminum foil for 30 minutes before serving (passage 3).\n\nNote that the exact cooking time may vary depending on the size of the roast, so adjust the cooking time accordingly. Also, make sure to check the internal temperature of the meat to ensure that it is cooked all the way through.", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.306, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.79, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "291", "pred": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "raw_output": "To cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven, preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack, with the fat side facing upwards. Cook the pork loin for approximately 20–30 minutes per pound. Use a meat thermometer to check the internal temperature; when it reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, remove the roast from the oven. Cover it with aluminum foil and let it rest for 30 minutes before serving.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 350 degrees Fahrenheit.\n2. Place the frozen pork loin in a shallow baking pan on top of a roasting rack with the fat of the pork loin facing upwards.\n3. Cook the pork loin for about 20-30 minutes per pound.\n4. Insert a meat thermometer and test the meat after cooking.\n5. When the internal temperature of the meat reaches 150 degrees Fahrenheit, cover the roast with aluminum foil and let it sit for 30 minutes before serving.\n\nSo, the steps to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven are: preheat the oven, place the roast in a shallow baking pan, cook for 20-30 minutes per pound, check the internal temperature, cover and let it sit before serving.", "question": "how to cook a frozen pork loin roast in the oven", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.137, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 65.79, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "294", "pred": "Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, use the correct fertilizer based on grass type and soil pH, apply in a perpendicular pattern, and check for over-fertilization by observing grass recovery after stepping on it.", "raw_output": "The proper way to fertilize your grass includes the following steps:\n\n1. Fertilize at the right time of year—specifically, at the beginning of the growing season. For warm-season grasses, this is when the grass starts to turn green in the spring.\n\n2. Choose the correct fertilizer based on your grass type and soil conditions. This involves knowing your grass type and conducting a pH soil test to determine the appropriate fertilizer pH.\n\n3. Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern to ensure even coverage.\n\n4. Check the health of your lawn after fertilizing by stepping on the turf. If the grass blades bounce back, the lawn is in good condition. If they lay flat, it indicates over-fertilization.\n\nAnswer: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, use the correct fertilizer based on grass type and soil pH, apply in a perpendicular pattern, and check for over-fertilization by observing grass recovery after stepping on it.", "gt": "To properly fertilize your grass, you should fertilize at the beginning of the growing season for your specific grass type. For warm season grasses, this would be when the grass turns green in the early spring. You should also know the type of fertilizer to use based on the type of grass you have and do a pH soil test to ensure the fertilizer has the appropriate pH for your soil. Once you've determined the correct fertilizer and timing, apply it in a perpendicular pattern and check the lawn after application by stepping on the turf. If the leaf blade bounces back up, the lawn is in good shape, but if it lays flat, you've over-fertilized.", "question": "what is the proper way to fertilize your grass", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 31.924, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 39.18, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "295", "pred": "Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, use the correct fertilizer based on grass type and soil pH, apply in a perpendicular pattern, and check for over-fertilization by observing grass recovery after stepping on it.", "raw_output": "The proper way to fertilize your grass includes the following steps:\n\n1. Fertilize at the right time of year—specifically, at the beginning of the growing season. For warm-season grasses, this is when the grass starts to turn green in the spring.\n\n2. Choose the correct fertilizer based on your grass type and soil conditions. This involves knowing your grass type and conducting a pH soil test to determine the appropriate fertilizer pH.\n\n3. Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern to ensure even coverage.\n\n4. Check the health of your lawn after fertilizing by stepping on the turf. If the grass blades bounce back, the lawn is in good condition. If they lay flat, it indicates over-fertilization.\n\nAnswer: Fertilize at the beginning of the growing season, use the correct fertilizer based on grass type and soil pH, apply in a perpendicular pattern, and check for over-fertilization by observing grass recovery after stepping on it.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the proper way to fertilize your grass is to:\n\n1. Fertilize at the right time of year, which is at the beginning of the growing season, and varies for different types of grasses.\n2. Choose a fertilizer that is suitable for your type of grass and soil pH, as determined by a soil test.\n3. Apply the fertilizer in a perpendicular pattern, avoiding over-fertilization, which can be determined by checking if the leaf blades bounce back up after being stepped on.", "question": "what is the proper way to fertilize your grass", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 32.05, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.84, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "296", "pred": "Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, which has a large amount of kinetic energy due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher, which has a large amount of kinetic energy despite its small mass because of its high velocity. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, which is the energy stored in an object due to its vertical position or height, such as a ball held at a height before being dropped.", "raw_output": "Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, which has a large amount of kinetic energy due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher, which has a large amount of kinetic energy despite its small mass because of its high velocity. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, which is the energy stored in an object due to its vertical position or height, such as a ball held at a height before being dropped.", "gt": "Examples of kinetic energy include an airplane in flight, due to its large mass and fast velocity, and a baseball thrown by a pitcher, which can have a large amount of kinetic energy due to its fast velocity. Examples of potential energy include gravitational potential energy, which is the energy stored in an object as the result of its vertical position or height, and elastic potential energy. Other activities like a car moving, a ball rolling, a river flowing, an athlete running, rides in an amusement park, and a satellite in orbit also involve potential and kinetic energy.", "question": "what are examples of potential and kinetic energy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 15.991, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 66.67, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
