{"id": "337", "pred": "To get rid of ticks from dogs, use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Pull the tick gently and steadily to remove it completely. Avoid yanking quickly, as this may leave part of the tick’s mouth behind, potentially causing an infection. It is recommended to wear gloves if using fingers to remove the tick. Additionally, check inside and behind the dog’s ears and around the eyes, as these are common tick hiding places. For prevention, use tick control products regularly, and consult your veterinarian to choose the best product for your dog and your area.", "raw_output": "To get rid of ticks from dogs, use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin. Pull the tick gently and steadily to remove it completely. Avoid yanking quickly, as this may leave part of the tick’s mouth behind, potentially causing an infection. It is recommended to wear gloves if using fingers to remove the tick. Additionally, check inside and behind the dog’s ears and around the eyes, as these are common tick hiding places. For prevention, use tick control products regularly, and consult your veterinarian to choose the best product for your dog and your area.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here's how to get rid of ticks from dogs:\n\n1. Check your dog's body thoroughly, especially in areas like inside and behind the ears, around the eyes, and other favorite tick hiding places.\n2. Use a pair of tweezers to grasp the head of the tick where it attaches to the skin, and wear gloves if needed.\n3. Pull on the tick gently and steadily, making sure not to yank it away from your dog too quickly.\n4. Regularly use tick control products on your dog, as recommended by your veterinarian, to prevent ticks from attaching to your dog in the first place.\n\nIf you have any further questions or concerns, consult your veterinarian for advice.", "question": "how to get rid of ticks from dogs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 21.518, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.9, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "338", "pred": "To make ribs, remove", "raw_output": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Prepare the ribs**: Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs using a knife to start it near the narrow end, then pull it off in one piece.\n\n2. **Season the ribs**: Lay the ribs on a rack in a single layer to allow heat circulation on all sides. Mix mustard with liquid smoke (if using) and brush it on both sides of the ribs. Then, sprinkle with a dry rub and gently pat it to ensure it adheres.\n\n3. **Cooking options**:\n   - **Grilling**: Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F). Remove ribs from foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush with sauce. Cover and cook for 5 minutes, flip, brush again, and cook another 5 minutes. Repeat every 5 minutes until the sauce thickens and the ribs are heated through with grill marks—total grilling time is about 20 minutes.\n   - **Oven cooking**: Place the ribs in a resealable bag with enough BBQ sauce to cover. Line a baking pan with heavy-duty foil, creating a tent with overlapping foil.\n\nAnswer: To make ribs, remove", "gt": "To make ribs, start by removing the membrane from the bottom of the ribs with a knife. Place the ribs in a resealable bag and add sufficient BBQ sauce to cover them. Afterwards, line a baking pan with heavy duty foil. Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer for heat circulation on all sides. Mix mustard and liquid smoke, if using, and brush the ribs on both sides. Sprinkle the ribs with a dry rub and pat gently to make sure it adheres to the meat. To grill the ribs, heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium and remove the ribs from the foil. Place them meaty-side up on the grill and brush them with some of the sauce. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes. Flip and brush the ribs and cook for another 5 minutes. Continue this process until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through with grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.", "question": "how to make ribs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.682, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.77, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "339", "pred": "To make ribs, remove", "raw_output": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Prepare the ribs**: Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs using a knife to start it near the narrow end, then pull it off in one piece.\n\n2. **Season the ribs**: Lay the ribs on a rack in a single layer to allow heat circulation on all sides. Mix mustard with liquid smoke (if using) and brush it on both sides of the ribs. Then, sprinkle with a dry rub and gently pat it to ensure it adheres.\n\n3. **Cooking options**:\n   - **Grilling**: Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F). Remove ribs from foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush with sauce. Cover and cook for 5 minutes, flip, brush again, and cook another 5 minutes. Repeat every 5 minutes until the sauce thickens and the ribs are heated through with grill marks—total grilling time is about 20 minutes.\n   - **Oven cooking**: Place the ribs in a resealable bag with enough BBQ sauce to cover. Line a baking pan with heavy-duty foil, creating a tent with overlapping foil.\n\nAnswer: To make ribs, remove", "gt": "To make ribs, start by removing the membrane from the bottom of the ribs. Then, brush both sides of the ribs with a mixture of mustard and liquid smoke. Sprinkle the ribs with a dry rub and pat gently to ensure it sticks to the meat. Next, heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium and place the ribs on the grill, meaty-side up. Brush the ribs with sauce, cover the grill, and cook for 5 minutes. Flip the ribs, brush with more sauce, and cook for another 5 minutes. Repeat this process, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce thickens and the ribs are heated through with grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.", "question": "how to make ribs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.705, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.41, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "340", "pred": "To make ribs, remove", "raw_output": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Prepare the ribs**: Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs using a knife to start it near the narrow end, then pull it off in one piece.\n\n2. **Season the ribs**: Lay the ribs on a rack in a single layer to allow heat circulation on all sides. Mix mustard with liquid smoke (if using) and brush it on both sides of the ribs. Then, sprinkle with a dry rub and gently pat it to ensure it adheres.\n\n3. **Cooking options**:\n   - **Grilling**: Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F). Remove ribs from foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush with sauce. Cover and cook for 5 minutes, flip, brush again, and cook another 5 minutes. Repeat every 5 minutes until the sauce thickens and the ribs are heated through with grill marks—total grilling time is about 20 minutes.\n   - **Oven cooking**: Place the ribs in a resealable bag with enough BBQ sauce to cover. Line a baking pan with heavy-duty foil, creating a tent with overlapping foil.\n\nAnswer: To make ribs, remove", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make ribs:\n\nTo grill the ribs:\n\n1. Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium temperature (350°F to 450°F).\n2. Remove the ribs from the foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush them with some of the sauce.\n3. Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes.\n4. Flip and brush the ribs and cook for another 5 minutes.\n5. Continue to cook, flipping and brushing every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through and have grill marks, for a total grilling time of 20 minutes.\n\nTo prepare the ribs before grilling:\n\n1. Lay the ribs on top of the rack in a single layer.\n2. Season the ribs by mixing the mustard and liquid smoke (if using), and brushing the ribs on both sides.\n3. Sprinkle the ribs with the dry rub and pat gently to make sure the rub adheres to the rib meat.\n\nFor oven-cooked BBQ ribs:\n\n1. Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs.\n2. Put the ribs in a resealable bag.\n3. Add sufficient BBQ sauce to cover the ribs.\n4. Line the baking pan with heavy-duty foil.\n5. Place the ribs in the baking pan and bake at 350°F for 2-3 hours, or until the ribs are tender and the meat starts to pull away from the bone.", "question": "how to make ribs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.612, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 3.97, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "341", "pred": "To make ribs, remove", "raw_output": "To make ribs, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Prepare the ribs**: Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs using a knife to start it near the narrow end, then pull it off in one piece.\n\n2. **Season the ribs**: Lay the ribs on a rack in a single layer to allow heat circulation on all sides. Mix mustard with liquid smoke (if using) and brush it on both sides of the ribs. Then, sprinkle with a dry rub and gently pat it to ensure it adheres.\n\n3. **Cooking options**:\n   - **Grilling**: Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium (350°F to 450°F). Remove ribs from foil, place them meaty-side up on the grill, and brush with sauce. Cover and cook for 5 minutes, flip, brush again, and cook another 5 minutes. Repeat every 5 minutes until the sauce thickens and the ribs are heated through with grill marks—total grilling time is about 20 minutes.\n   - **Oven cooking**: Place the ribs in a resealable bag with enough BBQ sauce to cover. Line a baking pan with heavy-duty foil, creating a tent with overlapping foil.\n\nAnswer: To make ribs, remove", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can make ribs:\n\nStep 1: Prepare the ribs\n\n* Remove the membrane from the bottom of the ribs (passage 3)\n* Season the ribs with a dry rub and mustard mixture (passage 2)\n\nStep 2: Grill the ribs\n\n* Heat a gas or charcoal grill to medium heat (passage 1)\n* Place the ribs meaty-side up on the grill and brush with some of the sauce (passage 1)\n* Cover the grill and cook for 5 minutes, then flip and brush the ribs with more sauce (passage 1)\n* Continue to cook and flip every 5 minutes, until the sauce has thickened and the ribs are heated through (passage 1)\n\nTotal grilling time: 20 minutes\n\nNote: The passages do not mention the temperature at which to cook the ribs, but a medium heat of 350°F to 450°F is suggested.", "question": "how to make ribs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 41.643, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.19, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "342", "pred": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "raw_output": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "gt": "While you may technically lose a small amount of weight after a bowel movement, this does not come from a loss of body fat. The decrease in body weight does not come from fat loss in stools, but rather from the elimination of undigested food and waste. Weight loss is primarily tied to your caloric intake and expenditure, not bowel movements. Unless your scale is highly accurate, you're unlikely to notice a difference in your body weight before and after a bowel movement.", "question": "when you have a bowel movement do you lose weight", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 16.158, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 67.8, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "343", "pred": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "raw_output": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it is clear that having a bowel movement does not result in weight loss. Weight loss is primarily determined by caloric intake and expenditure, not by bowel movements.", "question": "when you have a bowel movement do you lose weight", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 16.165, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "344", "pred": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "raw_output": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the answer is: No, having a bowel movement does not cause weight loss. The passages state that body weight loss comes from creating a calorie deficit, not from losing body fat in stools. Additionally, the fat in stools is not derived from body fat, but rather from dietary fat intake that is not properly absorbed. Therefore, hopping on the bathroom scale before and after a bowel movement will not accurately reflect any weight loss due to the bowel movement itself.", "question": "when you have a bowel movement do you lose weight", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 16.18, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.54, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "345", "pred": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "raw_output": "Yes, you may lose a small amount of weight when you have a bowel movement, but this is not due to the loss of body fat. The weight loss is from the elimination of waste material from your body, not from fat burning. According to the passage, unless your scale is highly accurate, you likely won't notice a difference in your body weight after a bowel movement. Body fat loss occurs through a calorie deficit, not from bowel movements.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the answer to the question \"When you have a bowel movement, do you lose weight?\" is: No, having a bowel movement does not result in a significant loss of body weight or body fat. The weight loss associated with having a bowel movement is minimal and temporary, and it is not a reliable indicator of weight loss or fat loss. Instead, weight loss occurs through a calorie deficit, which forces the body to burn stored fat for energy.", "question": "when you have a bowel movement do you lose weight", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 16.135, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 48.21, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "350", "pred": "The War brought economic unity to the U.S., integrated the South into the national market system, and established industrialization. Socially, African Americans were freed but faced segregation and economic dependency through crop lien contracts. Politically, Reconstruction involved reintegration of seceded states and changes to maintain national unity.", "raw_output": "The political, economic, and social effects on the United States include:\n\nEconomic effects:\n- The War brought economic unity to the U.S. by integrating the South into the national market system, ending its previous semi-subsistence economy.\n- Industrialization and large-scale economic enterprises became established and permanent features of the U.S. economy.\n\nSocial effects:\n- After the Civil War, African Americans were legally free but faced significant social challenges, including segregation and lack of employment opportunities.\n- Many African Americans were forced into crop lien work contracts, a system that kept them economically dependent on their former enslavers.\n\nPolitical effects:\n- The period of Reconstruction involved efforts to reintegrate the seceded states into the Union and address unresolved political issues.\n- Important political changes occurred to maintain national unity following the Civil War.\n\nAnswer: The War brought economic unity to the U.S., integrated the South into the national market system, and established industrialization. Socially, African Americans were freed but faced segregation and economic dependency through crop lien contracts. Politically, Reconstruction involved reintegration of seceded states and changes to maintain national unity.", "gt": "The passages provided offer some insights into the political, economic, and social effects of various events in the history of the United States.\n\nPassage 1 highlights the impact of immigration on the country, indicating that it has shaped the nation since its early days. Immigration has contributed to the diversity of the population, cultural exchange, and the development of the economy.\n\nPassage 2 discusses the long-term effects of Reconstruction, which include the continuation of racial discrimination, segregation, and unequal access to employment opportunities for African Americans. This period saw the introduction of crop lien work contracts, which kept many African Americans in a state of virtual slavery.\n\nPassage 3 discusses the economic and political effects of the Civil War. The war brought economic unity to the country, as the South transitioned from a semi-subsistence economy to a market-based economy, and industrialization gained momentum. The passage also notes that important political changes accompanied the maintenance of national unity.\n\nBased on these passages, we can infer that the political, economic, and social effects of these historical events in the United States include:\n\n1. Immigration: Shaping the nation's population, culture, and economy.\n2. Reconstruction: Continued racial discrimination, segregation, and unequal access to employment opportunities for African Americans.\n3. Civil War: Economic unity, industrialization, and political changes that maintained national unity.\n\nTherefore, the political, economic, and social effects of these events in the United States include increased diversity, economic growth, and the continued struggle for racial equality.", "question": "what political, economic and social effects have on the united states", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 37.04, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.3, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "351", "pred": "Goodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "raw_output": "Company goodwill refers to the intangible value of a business that arises from its reputation, customer relationships, and the trust customers have in its products or services. It is created when a business consistently provides good service or products, leading customers to become repeat buyers and recommend the business to others. This positive reputation and customer loyalty form an asset known as goodwill.\n\nGoodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "gt": "Company goodwill is a business asset that is created when a business provides good service or product to their customers, leading to customer trust. This trust results in repeat customers and positive word-of-mouth, which can increase a company's customer base and retain old clients. Goodwill represents the value of the attraction to the customers which the name and reputation of the business possesses. It is important because it can attract investors and future buyers, and it is an advantage of the reputation and connection with customers which tends to make that connection permanent.", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 21.276, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.86, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "353", "pred": "Goodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "raw_output": "Company goodwill refers to the intangible value of a business that arises from its reputation, customer relationships, and the trust customers have in its products or services. It is created when a business consistently provides good service or products, leading customers to become repeat buyers and recommend the business to others. This positive reputation and customer loyalty form an asset known as goodwill.\n\nGoodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, goodwill can be understood as a business asset that comes from a positive reputation and good connections with customers. It can be increased through providing good service or products, resulting in loyal and repeat customers who also spread positive reviews about the business. This goodwill attracts new customers, investors, and future buyers, making it an important factor in business success.", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 21.194, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.95, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "354", "pred": "Goodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "raw_output": "Company goodwill refers to the intangible value of a business that arises from its reputation, customer relationships, and the trust customers have in its products or services. It is created when a business consistently provides good service or products, leading customers to become repeat buyers and recommend the business to others. This positive reputation and customer loyalty form an asset known as goodwill.\n\nGoodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, I can confidently answer the following questions:\n\nWhat is company goodwill?\nCompany goodwill refers to the positive image and reputation of a business that is built over time through providing quality products or services, satisfying customers, and building strong relationships with them. Goodwill is a valuable asset for any business as it helps to attract new customers, retain existing ones, and attract investors.\n\nWhy is goodwill important in business?\nGoodwill is important in business because it helps to build trust and loyalty among customers, which leads to long-term relationships and repeat business. A strong reputation and goodwill can also make a business more attractive to potential investors and buyers. Additionally, goodwill can help a business to stand out from its competitors and establish a unique identity in the marketplace.", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 21.334, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 20.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "356", "pred": "Goodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "raw_output": "Company goodwill refers to the intangible value of a business that arises from its reputation, customer relationships, and the trust customers have in its products or services. It is created when a business consistently provides good service or products, leading customers to become repeat buyers and recommend the business to others. This positive reputation and customer loyalty form an asset known as goodwill.\n\nGoodwill is important because it helps increase a company's customer base, retains existing clients, attracts investors, and makes the business more appealing to future buyers. It represents the value derived from the business's name and reputation, which contributes to long-term customer attraction and business success.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, company goodwill refers to the positive feelings and loyalty of customers towards a business, gained through providing good services or products, building trust, and creating a strong reputation. Goodwill is important because it can increase customer retention, attract new customers, and make the business more attractive to investors and potential buyers.\n\nAccording to passage 3, goodwill is an intangible asset that represents the value of a business's reputation and customer connections, which can make the connection between the business and its customers permanent.\n\nTherefore, goodwill is important for businesses because it can lead to increased customer loyalty, positive word-of-mouth advertising, and higher sales, ultimately contributing to the long-term success and growth of the business.", "question": "what is company goodwill and why important", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 21.324, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 29.36, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "357", "pred": "In summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. This can take various forms, such as abusive ad hominem, where someone attacks the traits of an opponent to invalidate their argument.\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the argument is even presented. It involves discrediting or undermining a person's credibility (e.g., by questioning their integrity or intelligence) in advance, so that their subsequent arguments are dismissed without proper consideration.\n\nIn summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "gt": "An ad hominem fallacy occurs when someone attacks the character, motive, or other personal attribute of the person making an argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. This is often done to invalidate their arguments by associating their character with the soundness of their argument. On the other hand, the poisoning the well fallacy, which is a type of ad hominem attack, occurs when a person's integrity or intelligence is attacked before the merits of a case can be considered, effectively redirecting the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.749, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 30.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "358", "pred": "In summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. This can take various forms, such as abusive ad hominem, where someone attacks the traits of an opponent to invalidate their argument.\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the argument is even presented. It involves discrediting or undermining a person's credibility (e.g., by questioning their integrity or intelligence) in advance, so that their subsequent arguments are dismissed without proper consideration.\n\nIn summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy is that ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. On the other hand, poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered, specifically attacking a person's integrity or intelligence to redirect the discussion towards their faults.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.686, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.95, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "359", "pred": "In summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. This can take various forms, such as abusive ad hominem, where someone attacks the traits of an opponent to invalidate their argument.\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the argument is even presented. It involves discrediting or undermining a person's credibility (e.g., by questioning their integrity or intelligence) in advance, so that their subsequent arguments are dismissed without proper consideration.\n\nIn summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, an ad hominem fallacy is an attack on an argument made by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. On the other hand, poisoning the well fallacy is an ad hominem attack that takes place before the merits of a case can be considered and redirects a discussion to the faults of one of the parties. In summary, both types of attacks involve attacking the person making the argument, but poisoning the well fallacy specifically takes place before the merits of a case are considered.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.597, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.14, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "360", "pred": "In summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. This can take various forms, such as abusive ad hominem, where someone attacks the traits of an opponent to invalidate their argument.\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the argument is even presented. It involves discrediting or undermining a person's credibility (e.g., by questioning their integrity or intelligence) in advance, so that their subsequent arguments are dismissed without proper consideration.\n\nIn summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy:\n\nAd hominem fallacy refers to attacking an argument by attacking the character, motive, or other attribute of the person making the argument, rather than attacking the argument directly. Poisoning the well fallacy, on the other hand, is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the merits of a case can be considered, and it redirects the discussion to the faults of one of the parties.\n\nIn other words, ad hominem fallacy is a broader term that encompasses various types of attacks on the person making the argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that focuses on the person's integrity or intelligence.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.65, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.74, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "361", "pred": "In summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "raw_output": "The ad hominem fallacy involves attacking the character, motive, or other attributes of a person making an argument, rather than addressing the argument itself. This can take various forms, such as abusive ad hominem, where someone attacks the traits of an opponent to invalidate their argument.\n\nThe poisoning the well fallacy is a specific type of ad hominem attack that occurs before the argument is even presented. It involves discrediting or undermining a person's credibility (e.g., by questioning their integrity or intelligence) in advance, so that their subsequent arguments are dismissed without proper consideration.\n\nIn summary, while both fallacies involve attacking the person rather than the argument, the key difference is timing: ad hominem attacks happen during or after the argument, whereas poisoning the well happens before the argument is made.", "gt": "The main difference between ad hominem fallacy and poisoning the well fallacy is the timing of the attack. Ad hominem fallacy attacks the person making the argument instead of attacking the argument directly, but poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive attack on a person's character or integrity before they have even made their argument. In other words, ad hominem fallacy is a reaction to an argument, while poisoning the well fallacy is a preemptive strike to discredit an opponent before they have a chance to present their argument.", "question": "What’s the difference between ad hominem fallacy and the poisoning the well fallacy?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.635, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 34.57, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "364", "pred": "This technique allows you to duplicate the age-worn finishes seen in antiques quickly and effectively.", "raw_output": "To get an antique effect on wood using normal paint, follow these steps:\n\n1. Paint your wood piece with a basecoat of flat or satin paint. Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint gives an older look.\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than your basecoat with an equal amount of glaze.\n3. Once the basecoat dries, drip a crumpled rag or plastic bag into the glaze mixture to create a distressed, aged appearance.\n\nThis technique allows you to duplicate the age-worn finishes seen in antiques quickly and effectively.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here are some steps to achieve an antique effect on wood using normal paint:\n\n1. Apply a basecoat of flat or satin paint to the wood surface ( passage 3). Satin paint is easier to work with, but flat paint will give a more aged look.\n2. Mix paint that is two shades darker than the basecoat with an equal amount of glaze ( passage 3). Once the basecoat is dry, drizzle a crumpled rag or plastic bag in the glaze mixture to achieve an aged appearance.\n3. Layer stain on top of the paint to create a cool effect ( passage 1). This technique involves applying multiple layers of stain and allowing them to dry between coats.\n4. Use a palette knife to spread a mixture of molding paste and a rust or dull color on a canvas, letting it dry before adding additional layers ( passage 2). This can help create an embossed design with an antique finish.\n5. Apply a thin layer of burnt sienna or a similar color to give the wood a vintage look ( passage 3). Dabbing the paint with a paper towel or cloth can help remove excess paint and achieve a more subtle, aged appearance.\n\nOverall, the key to achieving an antique effect on wood using normal paint is to layer different materials and colors to create a weathered and worn appearance.", "question": "how to get antique effect on wood using normal paint", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 20.783, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 1.23, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "367", "pred": "In summary, the main difference lies in size and portability, with rocks being larger and heavier than stones.", "raw_output": "Based on the provided passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is primarily based on size and context:\n\n1. **Size and Portability**: A rock is described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to carry in the hand, whereas a stone is a smaller piece or pebble that can be easily carried in the hand.\n\n2. **Composition**: According to one passage, rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are derived from rocks.\n\n3. **Usage and Context**: While both terms are often used interchangeably, \"rock\" can also refer to something solid, substantial, or symbolic (e.g., \"the rock of one’s salvation\"), whereas \"stone\" more often implies human use or manipulation.\n\n4. **Functionality**: Stones can be pelted easily, but rocks cannot due to their size and weight.\n\nIn summary, the main difference lies in size and portability, with rocks being larger and heavier than stones.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, a rock is defined as the hard material of the earth's crust that is normally exposed on the surface or underlying the soil. On the other hand, a stone is described as a smaller piece or pebble that can be carried in the hand. The passages also mention that rocks are made of smaller stones and stones are made from rocks. Additionally, stones can be pelted easily, while rocks cannot. Therefore, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size, with rocks being larger and stones being smaller and easier to handle.", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 32.114, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 19.72, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "368", "pred": "In summary, the main difference lies in size and portability, with rocks being larger and heavier than stones.", "raw_output": "Based on the provided passages, the difference between a rock and a stone is primarily based on size and context:\n\n1. **Size and Portability**: A rock is described as a large piece of stone that is difficult to carry in the hand, whereas a stone is a smaller piece or pebble that can be easily carried in the hand.\n\n2. **Composition**: According to one passage, rocks are made of smaller stones, and stones are derived from rocks.\n\n3. **Usage and Context**: While both terms are often used interchangeably, \"rock\" can also refer to something solid, substantial, or symbolic (e.g., \"the rock of one’s salvation\"), whereas \"stone\" more often implies human use or manipulation.\n\n4. **Functionality**: Stones can be pelted easily, but rocks cannot due to their size and weight.\n\nIn summary, the main difference lies in size and portability, with rocks being larger and heavier than stones.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main difference between a rock and a stone is their size. Rocks are typically larger and more massive than stones, which are smaller and more fragmented.\n\nPassage 1 states that rocks are often used to refer to something solid, large, and grounded, such as a mountain or palisade, while stones are often used to refer to small pieces of rock or pebbles. Passage 2 defines rocks as the hard material of the earth's crust that is exposed on the surface of the earth or underlying the soil, while stones are smaller fragments of rock that can be carried in the hand.\n\nPassage 3 further clarifies the distinction by stating that rocks are made up of smaller stones, while stones are made from rocks. Additionally, rocks are described as being difficult to carry in the hand, while stones are small enough to be carried easily.\n\nIn summary, while both rocks and stones are made of the same material, they differ in size and usage. Rocks are generally larger and more massive, while stones are smaller and more fragmented.", "question": "what is the difference between a rock and a stone", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 31.969, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 13.45, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "375", "pred": "Additionally, DMSO has the ability to penetrate skin and tissue and enter the bloodstream, enhancing its effectiveness in topical applications.", "raw_output": "DMSO (dimethyl sulfoxide) is used for a variety of purposes:\n\n1. Industrial uses: It serves as an antifreeze and as a solvent for a wide range of chemicals.\n2. Medical uses: It is used for pain relief, particularly for muscles and joints, and reduces swelling, inflammation, and serves as a general pain reliever.\n3. Healing promotion: It is used for minor cuts and burns, and studies have shown its effectiveness in treating soft tissue damage, local tissue death, skin ulcers, and burns.\n4. Other medical applications: It has been used for conditions such as scleroderma and burns, and has been explored for various other medical ailments.\n\nAdditionally, DMSO has the ability to penetrate skin and tissue and enter the bloodstream, enhancing its effectiveness in topical applications.", "gt": "DMSO, or dimethyl sulfoxide, is used in a variety of ways both industrially and medically. Industrially, it is used as an antifreeze and a solvent for numerous chemicals. Medically, DMSO is applied to the skin as an anti-inflammatory and general pain reliever, most commonly for muscles and joints. It is also used to promote healing for minor cuts, burns, soft tissue damage, local tissue death, and skin ulcers. DMSO has the unique ability to penetrate skin and tissue, allowing it to enter the bloodstream.", "question": "what is dmso used for", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 27.945, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.62, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "380", "pred": "Additionally, DMSO has the ability to penetrate skin and tissue and enter the bloodstream, enhancing its effectiveness in topical applications.", "raw_output": "DMSO (dimethyl sulfoxide) is used for a variety of purposes:\n\n1. Industrial uses: It serves as an antifreeze and as a solvent for a wide range of chemicals.\n2. Medical uses: It is used for pain relief, particularly for muscles and joints, and reduces swelling, inflammation, and serves as a general pain reliever.\n3. Healing promotion: It is used for minor cuts and burns, and studies have shown its effectiveness in treating soft tissue damage, local tissue death, skin ulcers, and burns.\n4. Other medical applications: It has been used for conditions such as scleroderma and burns, and has been explored for various other medical ailments.\n\nAdditionally, DMSO has the ability to penetrate skin and tissue and enter the bloodstream, enhancing its effectiveness in topical applications.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, DMSO is used for various purposes, including:\n\n1. Industrial uses: As an antifreeze and solvent for a wide range of chemicals.\n2. Medical uses: To relieve pain, reduce swelling and inflammation, promote healing, and treat a variety of medical ailments such as burns, scleroderma, and soft tissue damage.\n3. Pain relief: For muscles and joints, and as a general pain reliever applied to the skin.\n4. Skin care: To penetrate skin and tissue and enter the bloodstream.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question \"What is DMSO used for?\" can be summarized as: DMSO is used for various industrial, medical, and personal care purposes, including pain relief, wound healing, and skin care.", "question": "what is dmso used for", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 27.992, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 13.33, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "381", "pred": "This will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the data you want to sort. You can click on the Select All button at the intersection of the row and column headings to select the entire worksheet, or highlight the specific range of cells you wish to sort.\n\n2. Go to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon.\n\n3. In the Sort & Filter group, click on the Sort option.\n\n4. In the Sort window, use the Sort By drop-down menu to select the column you want to sort by (e.g., the Product column).\n\n5. Choose the sorting order as alphabetical (A to Z) or reverse alphabetical (Z to A).\n\n6. Click OK to apply the sort.\n\nThis will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "gt": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, first click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings. Then, switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group. Click on the Sort option. Click on the Sort By drop-down menu to select a column by name. The names correspond to the headings typed in the first row of your data. Alternatively, you can use Kutools for Excel's Sort Sheets tool to sort all worksheets.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 27.544, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 9.52, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "382", "pred": "This will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the data you want to sort. You can click on the Select All button at the intersection of the row and column headings to select the entire worksheet, or highlight the specific range of cells you wish to sort.\n\n2. Go to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon.\n\n3. In the Sort & Filter group, click on the Sort option.\n\n4. In the Sort window, use the Sort By drop-down menu to select the column you want to sort by (e.g., the Product column).\n\n5. Choose the sorting order as alphabetical (A to Z) or reverse alphabetical (Z to A).\n\n6. Click OK to apply the sort.\n\nThis will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "gt": "Passage 3 provides the necessary information to answer the question. To arrange an excel sheet alphabetically, click on the Select All button, switch to the Data tab, locate the Sort & Filter group, click on the Sort option, and then select the desired column to sort by.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 27.641, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 15.79, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "383", "pred": "This will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the data you want to sort. You can click on the Select All button at the intersection of the row and column headings to select the entire worksheet, or highlight the specific range of cells you wish to sort.\n\n2. Go to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon.\n\n3. In the Sort & Filter group, click on the Sort option.\n\n4. In the Sort window, use the Sort By drop-down menu to select the column you want to sort by (e.g., the Product column).\n\n5. Choose the sorting order as alphabetical (A to Z) or reverse alphabetical (Z to A).\n\n6. Click OK to apply the sort.\n\nThis will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "gt": "To arrange an excel sheet alphabetical, one can follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the Data tab from the toolbar at the top of the screen.\n2. Click on the Sort button and select Custom Sort from the popup menu.\n3. When the Sort window appears, select the data that you wish to sort by. In this example, we want to sort by the Product column (column B) in alphabetical order (A to Z).\n4. Click on the OK button.\n5. The data should now be sorted in alphabetical order.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 27.546, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 5.88, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "384", "pred": "This will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the data you want to sort. You can click on the Select All button at the intersection of the row and column headings to select the entire worksheet, or highlight the specific range of cells you wish to sort.\n\n2. Go to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon.\n\n3. In the Sort & Filter group, click on the Sort option.\n\n4. In the Sort window, use the Sort By drop-down menu to select the column you want to sort by (e.g., the Product column).\n\n5. Choose the sorting order as alphabetical (A to Z) or reverse alphabetical (Z to A).\n\n6. Click OK to apply the sort.\n\nThis will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically:\n\nPassage 1 suggests using Kutools for Excel, which provides a Sort Sheets tool to easily sort all worksheets in alphabetical order. However, if you prefer not to use macros or Kutools, Passage 2 provides an alternative method using the built-in Sort feature in Excel.\n\nTo sort an Excel sheet alphabetically using the built-in Sort feature, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the data you wish to sort by clicking on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group.\n3. Click on the Sort option.\n4. Click on the Sort By drop-down menu and select the column you wish to sort by (e.g., Product column in alphabetical order - A to Z).\n5. Click OK.\n\nOnce you have completed these steps, the data in your Excel sheet will be sorted alphabetically.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages. The passages do not provide sufficient information to clarify how to arrange an Excel sheet numerically.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 27.357, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 7.14, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "385", "pred": "This will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "raw_output": "To arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, follow these steps:\n\n1. Select the data you want to sort. You can click on the Select All button at the intersection of the row and column headings to select the entire worksheet, or highlight the specific range of cells you wish to sort.\n\n2. Go to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon.\n\n3. In the Sort & Filter group, click on the Sort option.\n\n4. In the Sort window, use the Sort By drop-down menu to select the column you want to sort by (e.g., the Product column).\n\n5. Choose the sorting order as alphabetical (A to Z) or reverse alphabetical (Z to A).\n\n6. Click OK to apply the sort.\n\nThis will rearrange the data in the selected range alphabetically based on the chosen column.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how to arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically:\n\nPassage 2:\n\n1. Select the Data tab from the toolbar at the top of the screen.\n2. Click on the Sort button and select Custom Sort from the popup menu.\n3. Select the data that you wish to sort by (in this case, the Product column (column B)) and choose alphabetical order (A to Z).\n4. Click on the OK button.\n\nPassage 3:\n\n1. Click on the Select All button at the intersection of your row and column headings to select all the cells in the worksheet.\n2. Switch to the Data tab in the Microsoft Excel ribbon and locate the Sort & Filter group.\n3. Click on the Sort option.\n4. Click on the Sort By drop-down menu to select a column by name. The names correspond to the headings typed in the first row of your data.\n\nTherefore, to arrange an Excel sheet alphabetically, you can use either method described above.", "question": "how to arrange an excel sheet alphabetical", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 27.435, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 6.9, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "387", "pred": "The normal values for brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) are referred to as a reference range, which serves as a guide. However, the exact numerical values for the normal range are not specified in the provided passages.", "raw_output": "The normal values for brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) are referred to as a reference range, which serves as a guide. However, the exact numerical values for the normal range are not specified in the provided passages.", "gt": "The normal B-type Natriuretic Peptide (BNP) refers to a low amount of the BNP hormone found in the blood, produced by the heart as a measure of how well it is working. The exact normal values are not stated but are suggested to be a guide and can vary based on factors like age and gender, with values tending to increase with age and being higher in women. High levels of BNP are indicative of heart failure, with higher levels at diagnosis suggesting a more severe condition. Conversely, low levels tend to exclude heart failure.", "question": "what is the normal b natriuretic peptide", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 9.167, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 24.69, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
