{"id": "405", "pred": "The provided passages do not contain specific instructions for removing the door panel on a 2006 Chevy Impala. While passage 2 mentions that a video applies to the Chevy Impala model years 06, 07, 08, 09, 10, 11, and 12, it does not provide detailed steps for removing the door panel. Therefore, based on the information given, it is not possible to provide specific removal instructions for the 2006 Chevy door panel.", "raw_output": "Answer: The provided passages do not contain specific instructions for removing the door panel on a 2006 Chevy Impala. While passage 2 mentions that a video applies to the Chevy Impala model years 06, 07, 08, 09, 10, 11, and 12, it does not provide detailed steps for removing the door panel. Therefore, based on the information given, it is not possible to provide specific removal instructions for the 2006 Chevy door panel.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nTo remove the rear door panel in a 2006 Chevy HHR, you should follow these steps:\n\n1. Remove the two screws located inside the door pull cup.\n2. Remove the screw located in the middle of the inside door handle.\n3. Remove the pin on the outer edge of the door that holds the panel in place.\n4. Use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off of the HHR rear door.\n\nIt is important to note that an instructional video is also available that shows the entire process and location of the screws and pin.", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.497, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 25.21, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "406", "pred": "The provided passages do not contain specific instructions for removing the door panel on a 2006 Chevy Impala. While passage 2 mentions that a video applies to the Chevy Impala model years 06, 07, 08, 09, 10, 11, and 12, it does not provide detailed steps for removing the door panel. Therefore, based on the information given, it is not possible to provide specific removal instructions for the 2006 Chevy door panel.", "raw_output": "Answer: The provided passages do not contain specific instructions for removing the door panel on a 2006 Chevy Impala. While passage 2 mentions that a video applies to the Chevy Impala model years 06, 07, 08, 09, 10, 11, and 12, it does not provide detailed steps for removing the door panel. Therefore, based on the information given, it is not possible to provide specific removal instructions for the 2006 Chevy door panel.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is the answer to the question:\n\nTo remove the door panel on a 2006 Chevy, you will need to:\n\n1. Remove the two screws located inside the door pull cup.\n2. Remove the screw in the middle of the inside door handle.\n3. Remove the pin that holds the panel in place on the outer edge of the door.\n4. Use a door panel removal tool to pry the door panel off the rear door.\n5. Remove any wiring connections.\n6. Gently pull the panel away from the frame.\n\nAfter removing the screws and clips, you will be able to remove the door panel. It is recommended to watch an instructional video to see the process and locate the parts mentioned.", "question": "2006 chevy remove door panel", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.505, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.39, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "408", "pred": "Note: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "raw_output": "To make panko-style crumbs, follow these steps:\n\n1. Use a food processor to push torn pieces or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Alternatively, you can use a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form.\n2. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half-sheet pans.\n3. Preheat the oven to 300°F (150°C).\n4. Bake the crumbs for about 5 to 8 minutes, depending on the recipe, until they are dry and crisp but not toasted or browned.\n5. Shake the baking sheet(s) twice during baking to ensure even drying.\n6. Once done, cool the crumbs completely on the pans.\n7. Store the cooled crumbs in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nNote: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "gt": "To make panko crumbs, preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C). Push torn pieces or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake at 300 degrees F for 6 to 8 minutes, shaking the sheet twice during baking. Remove from the oven and cool completely.", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.95, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 11.94, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "409", "pred": "Note: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "raw_output": "To make panko-style crumbs, follow these steps:\n\n1. Use a food processor to push torn pieces or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Alternatively, you can use a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form.\n2. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half-sheet pans.\n3. Preheat the oven to 300°F (150°C).\n4. Bake the crumbs for about 5 to 8 minutes, depending on the recipe, until they are dry and crisp but not toasted or browned.\n5. Shake the baking sheet(s) twice during baking to ensure even drying.\n6. Once done, cool the crumbs completely on the pans.\n7. Store the cooled crumbs in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nNote: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make panko crumbs:\n\nPassage 1 states to push torn pieces of bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 2 provides similar instructions, stating to push chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs. Then, spread the crumbs on a baking sheet and bake at 300°F until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, about 6 to 8 minutes.\n\nPassage 3 provides additional information, suggesting to preheat the oven to 300°F before making the crumbs. The passage also recommends using a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form, and then spreading the crumbs evenly on two half-sheet pans and baking until they just begin to brown and are crisp, about 5 minutes. After cooling the crumbs on the pans for 15 to 20 minutes, transfer them to an airtight container and store them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nTherefore, to make panko crumbs based on the provided passages, you can use a food processor or blender to shred or pulse bread into coarse crumbs, and then bake them in a preheated oven until they are dry but not toasted.", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.987, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 8.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "410", "pred": "Note: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "raw_output": "To make panko-style crumbs, follow these steps:\n\n1. Use a food processor to push torn pieces or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Alternatively, you can use a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form.\n2. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half-sheet pans.\n3. Preheat the oven to 300°F (150°C).\n4. Bake the crumbs for about 5 to 8 minutes, depending on the recipe, until they are dry and crisp but not toasted or browned.\n5. Shake the baking sheet(s) twice during baking to ensure even drying.\n6. Once done, cool the crumbs completely on the pans.\n7. Store the cooled crumbs in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nNote: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how to make panko crumbs:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C).\n2. Torn pieces of bread are pushed through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs.\n3. The crumbs are spread on a baking sheet.\n4. The sheet is baked in the preheated oven until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, for 6 to 8 minutes.\n5. The sheet is shaken twice during baking to ensure even drying.\n6. After baking, the crumbs are removed from the oven and allowed to cool completely.\n\nAlternatively, according to passage 2, you can also make panko crumbs by:\n\n1. Pushing chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to create coarse crumbs.\n2. Spreading the crumbs on a baking sheet and baking at 300 degrees F until the crumbs are dry but not toasted, for 6 to 8 minutes.\n3. Shaking the sheet twice during baking.\n4. Being careful not to let the crumbs brown.\n\nFinally, passage 3 provides a third method for making panko crumbs, which involves blending the ingredients in a blender carafe and baking them on half sheet pans until they are crisp and lightly browned.", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.688, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 11.04, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "411", "pred": "Note: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "raw_output": "To make panko-style crumbs, follow these steps:\n\n1. Use a food processor to push torn pieces or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk to create coarse crumbs. Alternatively, you can use a blender carafe to pulse the bread until coarse crumbs form.\n2. Spread the crumbs evenly on a baking sheet or two half-sheet pans.\n3. Preheat the oven to 300°F (150°C).\n4. Bake the crumbs for about 5 to 8 minutes, depending on the recipe, until they are dry and crisp but not toasted or browned.\n5. Shake the baking sheet(s) twice during baking to ensure even drying.\n6. Once done, cool the crumbs completely on the pans.\n7. Store the cooled crumbs in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nNote: Unlike traditional panko, which is made from bread with a specific texture and is often lightly toasted, these methods create a similar light, crispy crumb using regular bread and oven drying.", "gt": "Sure, I can help you with that! Based on the given passages, here's a step-by-step guide on how to make panko crumbs:\n\n1. Preheat the oven to 300 degrees F (150 degrees C).\n2. Push torn pieces of bread or chunks of white bread through the shredding disk of a food processor to make coarse crumbs.\n3. Spread the crumbs on a baking sheet.\n4. Bake in preheated oven until crumbs are dry but not toasted, 6 to 8 minutes. Shake the sheet twice during baking to evenly dry the crumbs.\n5. Remove from the oven and cool completely.\n\nIf you want to store the panko crumbs for later use, you can transfer them to an airtight container and keep them at room temperature for up to 2 weeks.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "question": "how to make panko crumbs", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 34.777, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 9.76, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
