{"id": "492", "pred": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who has favorited your tweets. Additionally, you can set up your Twitter account to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite by going to your Twitter Settings, clicking on the Notifications link, and enabling the appropriate options.", "raw_output": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who has favorited your tweets. Additionally, you can set up your Twitter account to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite by going to your Twitter Settings, clicking on the Notifications link, and enabling the appropriate options.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, there are several ways to track who likes your tweets:\n\n1. Favotter.net and Favstar.fm - These websites allow you to track who has favorited your tweets and which tweets have been favorited by multiple users. (Passage 1)\n2. Using a web-based website - There are websites that allow you to check Twitter users who favorite tweets. (Passage 2)\n3. On Twitter - You can set up your account settings to receive notifications via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the answer to the question is: You can use Favotter.net, Favstar.fm, or a web-based website to track who likes your tweets, or you can set up your Twitter account settings to receive email notifications.", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.37, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 50.72, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "493", "pred": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who has favorited your tweets. Additionally, you can set up your Twitter account to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite by going to your Twitter Settings, clicking on the Notifications link, and enabling the appropriate options.", "raw_output": "You can tell who liked your tweet by using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, which track who has favorited your tweets. Additionally, you can set up your Twitter account to receive email notifications whenever someone marks one of your tweets as a favorite by going to your Twitter Settings, clicking on the Notifications link, and enabling the appropriate options.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how you can tell who liked your tweet:\n\nPassage 1 suggests using websites such as Favotter.net and Favstar.fm to track who has favorited your tweets. These websites show you a list of users who have favorited your tweets, allowing you to see who has shown interest in your content.\n\nPassage 2 provides a step-by-step guide on how to track who favorited your tweets. According to the passage, you can use a simple web-based website to check who favorited your tweets. Once you have access to this website, you can enter your tweet's URL to view a list of users who have favorited it.\n\nPassage 3 does not provide any information on tracking who favorited your tweets. However, it does mention that you can set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.\n\nIn summary, to tell who liked your tweet, you can use websites like Favotter.net and Favstar.fm, or set up your Twitter account to notify you via email whenever someone marks one of your tweets as one of their favorites.", "question": "how can you tell who liked your tweet", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 13.391, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.67, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "495", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a scheme where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement is also known as salary sacrifice. By reducing their taxable salary, employees can lower their income tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs), while employers also benefit from reduced NICs payments. The amount waived by the employee is typically equal to their regular pension contribution, such as 7.5% of pay for USS members (from 1 October 2011) or 9% for USPAS members (from 1 August 2011), excluding any additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). In return, the employer contributes the same amount into the employee's pension scheme. This system allows both parties to save on taxes and NICs.", "raw_output": "Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a scheme where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement is also known as salary sacrifice. By reducing their taxable salary, employees can lower their income tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs), while employers also benefit from reduced NICs payments. The amount waived by the employee is typically equal to their regular pension contribution, such as 7.5% of pay for USS members (from 1 October 2011) or 9% for USPAS members (from 1 August 2011), excluding any additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). In return, the employer contributes the same amount into the employee's pension scheme. This system allows both parties to save on taxes and NICs.", "gt": "Salary exchange pension contributions, also known as salary sacrifice, is a method where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment paid by their employer. This can allow both employers and employees to save money. The salary that is waived under the salary exchange is equal to the value of the regular pension contribution made. For example, it could be 7.5% or 9% of the pay, depending on the pension scheme. The employer then pays a corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme.", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 28.288, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 54.67, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "496", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a scheme where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement is also known as salary sacrifice. By reducing their taxable salary, employees can lower their income tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs), while employers also benefit from reduced NICs payments. The amount waived by the employee is typically equal to their regular pension contribution, such as 7.5% of pay for USS members (from 1 October 2011) or 9% for USPAS members (from 1 August 2011), excluding any additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). In return, the employer contributes the same amount into the employee's pension scheme. This system allows both parties to save on taxes and NICs.", "raw_output": "Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a scheme where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement is also known as salary sacrifice. By reducing their taxable salary, employees can lower their income tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs), while employers also benefit from reduced NICs payments. The amount waived by the employee is typically equal to their regular pension contribution, such as 7.5% of pay for USS members (from 1 October 2011) or 9% for USPAS members (from 1 August 2011), excluding any additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). In return, the employer contributes the same amount into the employee's pension scheme. This system allows both parties to save on taxes and NICs.", "gt": "Salary exchange pension contributions allow employees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension payment made by their employer. This arrangement benefits both employers and employees as employers pay lower National Insurance Contributions (NICs) due to the reduced salary, and employees can save money by making pension contributions through this method. The amount of salary waived under the salary exchange is equivalent to the regular pension contribution, and the employer pays a corresponding amount into the pension scheme. The specific details of the contributions may vary depending on the pension scheme being used.", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 28.199, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.83, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "497", "pred": "Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a scheme where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement is also known as salary sacrifice. By reducing their taxable salary, employees can lower their income tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs), while employers also benefit from reduced NICs payments. The amount waived by the employee is typically equal to their regular pension contribution, such as 7.5% of pay for USS members (from 1 October 2011) or 9% for USPAS members (from 1 August 2011), excluding any additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). In return, the employer contributes the same amount into the employee's pension scheme. This system allows both parties to save on taxes and NICs.", "raw_output": "Salary exchange pension contributions refer to a scheme where an employee agrees to give up part of their salary or bonus in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. This arrangement is also known as salary sacrifice. By reducing their taxable salary, employees can lower their income tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs), while employers also benefit from reduced NICs payments. The amount waived by the employee is typically equal to their regular pension contribution, such as 7.5% of pay for USS members (from 1 October 2011) or 9% for USPAS members (from 1 August 2011), excluding any additional voluntary contributions (AVCs). In return, the employer contributes the same amount into the employee's pension scheme. This system allows both parties to save on taxes and NICs.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that salary exchange is a way for employees to give up part of their salary in exchange for a pension contribution made by their employer. The salary that is waived is equal to the value of the employee's regular pension contribution, minus any additional contributions. The employer then pays the corresponding amount into the relevant pension scheme. This arrangement allows both employers and employees to potentially save money through reduced national insurance contributions.", "question": "salary exchange pension contributions", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 28.364, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.11, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "501", "pred": "Factors that cause oily skin include genetics and hormonal shifts. According to the passage, oily skin is hereditary, meaning if your parents have oily skin, you are likely to have it as well. Additionally, if oily skin fluctuates, it may be due to hormonal changes in the body rather than genetic factors. While diet and lifestyle are often blamed, they do not cause oily skin—though diet can aggravate acne. Poor hygiene and topical applications may also contribute to the appearance of oiliness, but the primary causes are genetic and hormonal.", "raw_output": "Factors that cause oily skin include genetics and hormonal shifts. According to the passage, oily skin is hereditary, meaning if your parents have oily skin, you are likely to have it as well. Additionally, if oily skin fluctuates, it may be due to hormonal changes in the body rather than genetic factors. While diet and lifestyle are often blamed, they do not cause oily skin—though diet can aggravate acne. Poor hygiene and topical applications may also contribute to the appearance of oiliness, but the primary causes are genetic and hormonal.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the factors that cause oily skin are primarily genetic/hereditary factors and hormonal shifts in the body. Diet and poor hygiene/topical applications have a limited effect on the oiliness of the skin. Combination skin, which is a mix of oily and dry/normal areas, can also contribute to oily skin in certain areas such as the T-zone.", "question": "factors that cause oily skin", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.178, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.42, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "503", "pred": "Factors that cause oily skin include genetics and hormonal shifts. According to the passage, oily skin is hereditary, meaning if your parents have oily skin, you are likely to have it as well. Additionally, if oily skin fluctuates, it may be due to hormonal changes in the body rather than genetic factors. While diet and lifestyle are often blamed, they do not cause oily skin—though diet can aggravate acne. Poor hygiene and topical applications may also contribute to the appearance of oiliness, but the primary causes are genetic and hormonal.", "raw_output": "Factors that cause oily skin include genetics and hormonal shifts. According to the passage, oily skin is hereditary, meaning if your parents have oily skin, you are likely to have it as well. Additionally, if oily skin fluctuates, it may be due to hormonal changes in the body rather than genetic factors. While diet and lifestyle are often blamed, they do not cause oily skin—though diet can aggravate acne. Poor hygiene and topical applications may also contribute to the appearance of oiliness, but the primary causes are genetic and hormonal.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, the main factors that contribute to oily skin are:\n\n1. Genetics: According to passage 1, oily skin is hereditary, meaning that if one's parents have oily skin, they are more likely to experience it themselves.\n2. Hormonal shifts: Passage 1 suggests that hormonal changes in the body can cause oily skin to come and go.\n3. Diet: While diet can aggravate acne, it does not directly cause oily skin, according to passage 1.\n4. Poor hygiene: Passage 2 mentions that poor hygiene and topical applications can contribute to oily skin.\n5. Combination skin: Passage 3 defines different skin types, including combination skin, which can be prone to oily skin in certain areas, such as the T-zone.\n\nThere is no additional information provided in the passages to suggest other factors that might contribute to oily skin. Therefore, the answer to the question is based on the information provided in the passages.", "question": "factors that cause oily skin", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 19.103, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 45.09, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "504", "pred": "The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms. Due to the heat and moisture in the biome, dead plants and animals rot quickly, providing rich nutrients that support the growth of other plants.", "raw_output": "The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms. Due to the heat and moisture in the biome, dead plants and animals rot quickly, providing rich nutrients that support the growth of other plants.", "gt": "The floor of a tropical rainforest biome is characterized by a fast rate of decay due to the heat and moisture. This decay provides rich nutrients for other plants to grow. Various types of fruits, nuts, herbs, and mushrooms grow on the rainforest floor. Additionally, vines and ferns grow all over the rainforest, including the floor, helping to connect the various layers of the biome.", "question": "what are the characteristics of tropical rainforest floor", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 8.824, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.75, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "507", "pred": "The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms. Due to the heat and moisture in the biome, dead plants and animals rot quickly, providing rich nutrients that support the growth of other plants.", "raw_output": "The tropical rainforest floor is characterized by the presence of herbs and mushrooms. Due to the heat and moisture in the biome, dead plants and animals rot quickly, providing rich nutrients that support the growth of other plants.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the characteristics of the tropical rainforest floor are:\n\n1. High levels of rainfall, with some areas receiving up to 400 inches of rain annually.\n2. High humidity due to the large amount of rainfall.\n3. Abundant vegetation, including tall trees, vines, ferns, herbs, mushrooms, fruits, and nuts.\n4. Quick decay of plant and animal matter due to the heat and moisture, which provides rich nutrients for other plants to grow.\n5. Presence of bananas growing on vines.\n\nTherefore, the tropical rainforest floor is characterized by high rainfall, humidity, and lush vegetation, with a diverse array of plant species and a rapid rate of decay and nutrient cycling.", "question": "what are the characteristics of tropical rainforest floor", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 8.904, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 28.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "508", "pred": "The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak lies in their cut location and composition. A sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, continuing off the short loin. In contrast, a porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin area that includes a large portion of the tenderloin (fillet) on one side of a T-shaped bone and part of the strip steak (top loin) on the other. The porterhouse is essentially a larger version of the T-bone steak, with more tenderloin meat than a T-bone, and is considered a premium cut due to its size and quality.", "raw_output": "The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak lies in their cut location and composition. A sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, continuing off the short loin. In contrast, a porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin area that includes a large portion of the tenderloin (fillet) on one side of a T-shaped bone and part of the strip steak (top loin) on the other. The porterhouse is essentially a larger version of the T-bone steak, with more tenderloin meat than a T-bone, and is considered a premium cut due to its size and quality.", "gt": "Porterhouse steaks are cut from further back on the sirloin and contain more of the tenderloin or fillet than sirloin steaks. They are essentially a larger version of the T-Bone steak, carved from a larger portion of the tenderloin. On the other hand, the sirloin steak is a steak cut from the back of the animal, continuing off the short loin from which T-bone, porterhouse, and club steaks are cut. The sirloin is divided into several different types of steak.", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.938, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 52.63, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "509", "pred": "The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak lies in their cut location and composition. A sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, continuing off the short loin. In contrast, a porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin area that includes a large portion of the tenderloin (fillet) on one side of a T-shaped bone and part of the strip steak (top loin) on the other. The porterhouse is essentially a larger version of the T-bone steak, with more tenderloin meat than a T-bone, and is considered a premium cut due to its size and quality.", "raw_output": "The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak lies in their cut location and composition. A sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, continuing off the short loin. In contrast, a porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin area that includes a large portion of the tenderloin (fillet) on one side of a T-shaped bone and part of the strip steak (top loin) on the other. The porterhouse is essentially a larger version of the T-bone steak, with more tenderloin meat than a T-bone, and is considered a premium cut due to its size and quality.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is that porterhouse steak is a larger version of T-Bone steak and it contains more of the tenderloin or fillet than T-Bone steak.", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.961, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 40.0, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "510", "pred": "The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak lies in their cut location and composition. A sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, continuing off the short loin. In contrast, a porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin area that includes a large portion of the tenderloin (fillet) on one side of a T-shaped bone and part of the strip steak (top loin) on the other. The porterhouse is essentially a larger version of the T-bone steak, with more tenderloin meat than a T-bone, and is considered a premium cut due to its size and quality.", "raw_output": "The difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak lies in their cut location and composition. A sirloin steak is cut from the rear back portion of the animal, continuing off the short loin. In contrast, a porterhouse steak is a specific cut from the short loin area that includes a large portion of the tenderloin (fillet) on one side of a T-shaped bone and part of the strip steak (top loin) on the other. The porterhouse is essentially a larger version of the T-bone steak, with more tenderloin meat than a T-bone, and is considered a premium cut due to its size and quality.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, it appears that the main difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak is their location within the sirloin muscle. Sirloin steaks are cut from the back of the animal and continue off the short loin from which other cuts such as T-bone and porterhouse steaks are made. On the other hand, porterhouse steaks are specifically carved from the larger portion of the tenderloin and therefore have more of the tenderloin or fillet compared to T-bone steaks. However, the passages suggest that both sirloin and porterhouse steaks may be referred to as the same thing or as a large version of the T-bone due to their similarity in appearance and taste.", "question": "what is the difference between sirloin steak and porterhouse steak", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.841, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 46.27, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "516", "pred": "The word \"Gemini\" is derived from Latin, where it means \"twins.\" It is one of the constellations that visually resemble its name, depicting two figures that represent twins. The first known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica*, over 300 years before Christ, where he observed an occultation of a star in Gemini and noted Jupiter's conjunction with it. In astrology, Gemini is the third sign of the zodiac, following Taurus, and occupies the third 30-degree segment of the zodiacal circle. It is a mutable sign associated with change, marking the transition from spring to summer. The mythology behind Gemini is rooted in Greek mythology, where the twins are Castor and Pollux, sons of Leda but with different fathers—Castor being the son of Tyndarus, King of Sparta, and Pollux being the son of Zeus.", "raw_output": "The word \"Gemini\" is derived from Latin, where it means \"twins.\" It is one of the constellations that visually resemble its name, depicting two figures that represent twins. The first known reference to the Gemini constellation appears in Aristotle’s *Meteorologica*, over 300 years before Christ, where he observed an occultation of a star in Gemini and noted Jupiter's conjunction with it. In astrology, Gemini is the third sign of the zodiac, following Taurus, and occupies the third 30-degree segment of the zodiacal circle. It is a mutable sign associated with change, marking the transition from spring to summer. The mythology behind Gemini is rooted in Greek mythology, where the twins are Castor and Pollux, sons of Leda but with different fathers—Castor being the son of Tyndarus, King of Sparta, and Pollux being the son of Zeus.", "gt": "Sure! Here's a brief history of the word Gemini based on the given passages:\n\nThe word \"Gemini\" comes from Latin, meaning \"twins.\" The constellation Gemini was first mentioned in Aristotle's Meteorologica over 300 years BC. In Greek mythology, the twins represented in Gemini are Castor and Pollux, who share the same mother, Leda, but have different fathers. The constellation Gemini is not in the same position as the constellation of Gemini and is a mutable sign that announces change and rules the time of year when Taurus spring has ended.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you need further clarification or if there's anything else I can help you with.", "question": "history of the word gemini", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 31.378, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 33.13, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "518", "pred": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket representing a chance to win a prize. In a traditional raffle, such as the reverse raffle described, participants receive numbered tickets (e.g., from 1 to 55), and prizes are awarded based on a random draw. In the reverse raffle, the process is \"backwards\"—tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total ticket sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is donated to charity. Other raffles, like the cash tip board raffle mentioned, may offer additional prize opportunities outside the main ticket price.", "raw_output": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket representing a chance to win a prize. In a traditional raffle, such as the reverse raffle described, participants receive numbered tickets (e.g., from 1 to 55), and prizes are awarded based on a random draw. In the reverse raffle, the process is \"backwards\"—tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total ticket sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is donated to charity. Other raffles, like the cash tip board raffle mentioned, may offer additional prize opportunities outside the main ticket price.", "gt": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, who have the opportunity to win prizes. The specific details and mechanics of a raffle can vary. In a reverse raffle, participants purchase tickets with numbers on them, and the last ticket drawn wins the main prize. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the proceeds go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is allocated for a charitable purpose. In some cases, raffles may also offer additional opportunities to win prizes, such as cash tip board raffles.", "question": "how do raffles work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.942, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.14, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "519", "pred": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket representing a chance to win a prize. In a traditional raffle, such as the reverse raffle described, participants receive numbered tickets (e.g., from 1 to 55), and prizes are awarded based on a random draw. In the reverse raffle, the process is \"backwards\"—tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total ticket sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is donated to charity. Other raffles, like the cash tip board raffle mentioned, may offer additional prize opportunities outside the main ticket price.", "raw_output": "Raffles work by selling tickets to participants, with each ticket representing a chance to win a prize. In a traditional raffle, such as the reverse raffle described, participants receive numbered tickets (e.g., from 1 to 55), and prizes are awarded based on a random draw. In the reverse raffle, the process is \"backwards\"—tickets are drawn one by one, and the last remaining ticket wins. In a 50-50 draw, tickets are sold during an event, and 50% of the total ticket sales go to the winner of a random draw, while the other 50% is donated to charity. Other raffles, like the cash tip board raffle mentioned, may offer additional prize opportunities outside the main ticket price.", "gt": "Based on the provided passages, here is how raffles work:\n\n1. Reverse Raffle: In this type of raffle, the numbers on the entry tickets are drawn in reverse order, starting with the last number and ending with the first number. For example, if there are 55 entries, the last number drawn wins the grand prize, followed by the next-to-last number, and so on until the first number. Each entry ticket costs $50, and a table of 20 individuals constitutes one complete ticket worth $1,000 and two $500 prizes (for the early bird drawing and the first ticket pulled).\n2. 50-50 Draw: This is a raffle held during a single day at a specific event. Tickets are sold to patrons throughout the event by roving staff with handheld electronic devices. The gross proceeds of 50-50 tickets sold at the event go into a prize pool, and 50% of the prize pool is awarded to the winner of a random draw that takes place at the end of the event. The remaining 50% goes to a charitable purpose chosen by the sporting club.\n3. Additional Raffles: In addition to the main raffle, there may be additional raffles for drinks and dinner. These raffles are not included in the original ticket price and require separate entry fees. One of these raffles offers the chance to win $10,000!\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "how do raffles work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 26.934, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 42.4, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
