{"id": "530", "pred": "E-ZPass charges work by reading the toll tag mounted on your vehicle's windshield as it enters the toll lane. The system automatically deducts the proper toll amount from your E-ZPass account. If your vehicle does not have a toll tag, the system classifies you as a violator and takes photos of your license plate for processing.", "raw_output": "E-ZPass charges work by reading the toll tag mounted on your vehicle's windshield as it enters the toll lane. The system automatically deducts the proper toll amount from your E-ZPass account. If your vehicle does not have a toll tag, the system classifies you as a violator and takes photos of your license plate for processing.", "gt": "Sure! Based on the given passages, here is how E-ZPass charging works:\n\nWhen you sign up for E-ZPass, a certain amount is charged to your credit card to establish a balance (specifically, $100 for the EZ-Tag and $25 for the TX-Tag). This balance is used to pay for tolls as you drive through toll lanes. When you drive through a toll booth, the toll tag mounted on your windshield is read by the antennae, and your E-ZPass account is charged the appropriate amount. The amount is deducted from your balance. If your balance falls below a certain threshold ($10 for the TX-Tag), E-ZPass will charge your credit card an additional $100 or $25 to top up your balance.", "question": "how does ez pass charges work", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 12.557, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 26.89, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
