e into the lungs, causing us to inhale. (Passage 3)\n\nTherefore, the main steps in processing the air we breathe before it reaches the lungs are:\n\n1. Contraction of the diaphragm muscle to draw air into the lungs.\n2. Exchange of gases between the body and the outside environment in the lungs.\n3. Expansion of the thoracic cavity to decrease air pressure in the lungs and facilitate inhalation.\n\nUnable to answer based on given passages.", "question": "how do you process the air you breathe before it gets to your lungs?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.47, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 43.96, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "733", "pred": "The air you breathe is processed through the process of ventilation, which involves the movement of air into the lungs during inhalation and out of the lungs during exhalation. When you inhale, the diaphragm muscle contracts and moves downward from the bottom of the rib cage toward the abdominal region. This action increases the volume of the thoracic cavity, which in turn increases the volume of the lungs. As the lung volume increases, the air pressure inside the lungs decreases below atmospheric pressure, causing air to be drawn from the atmosphere into the lungs. This process allows oxygen-rich air to enter the lungs, where gas exchange with the blood occurs.", "raw_output": "Answer: The air you breathe is processed through the process of ventilation, which involves the movement of air into the lungs during inhalation and out of the lungs during exhalation. When you inhale, the diaphragm muscle contracts and moves downward from the bottom of the rib cage toward the abdominal region. This action increases the volume of the thoracic cavity, which in turn increases the volume of the lungs. As the lung volume increases, the air pressure inside the lungs decreases below atmospheric pressure, causing air to be drawn from the atmosphere into the lungs. This process allows oxygen-rich air to enter the lungs, where gas exchange with the blood occurs.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, here is how the air you breathe is processed before it reaches your lungs:\n\nWhen you inhale, the diaphragm muscle contracts, moving down from the bottom of your rib cage towards your abdominal region, which causes the thoracic cavity to increase in volume. This decrease in air pressure in the lungs below atmospheric pressure forces air from the atmosphere into the lungs, causing you to inhale.\n\nTherefore, the air you breathe is processed by the diaphragm muscle before it reaches your lungs, which contracts to take air into the lungs and relax to let it out again. Additionally, the process of breathing involves the exchange of gases, such as oxygen and carbon dioxide, between the body and the outside environment, which occurs in the lungs.", "question": "how do you process the air you breathe before it gets to your lungs?", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 22.361, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 55.48, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "735", "pred": "SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "raw_output": "SMS (Short Message Service) is a text messaging service that allows users to send text-only messages of up to 160 characters. Longer messages are automatically split into multiple parts. SMS is supported by most cell phones and is limited to plain text.\n\nMMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) is an evolution of SMS that allows users to send messages containing multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio files, and text in combination. MMS messages can carry larger content sizes, up to 300KB, and support formats like WAV, JPEG, and GIF.\n\nIn summary, the main difference is that SMS is limited to text, while MMS supports multimedia content.\n\nAnswer: SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "gt": "SMS stands for Short Message Service, commonly referred to as a text message, and allows you to send a text message of up to 160 characters to another device. MMS, which stands for Multimedia Messaging Service, is an evolution of SMS. It allows you to send messages that include pictures, video, or audio content to another device. MMS messages can carry a larger size of content up to 300KB. SMS is restricted to sending text-only messages, whereas MMS allows a combination of text, pictures, sounds, and videos.", "question": "what is the difference between sms and mms", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 31.597, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 47.62, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "736", "pred": "SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "raw_output": "SMS (Short Message Service) is a text messaging service that allows users to send text-only messages of up to 160 characters. Longer messages are automatically split into multiple parts. SMS is supported by most cell phones and is limited to plain text.\n\nMMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) is an evolution of SMS that allows users to send messages containing multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio files, and text in combination. MMS messages can carry larger content sizes, up to 300KB, and support formats like WAV, JPEG, and GIF.\n\nIn summary, the main difference is that SMS is limited to text, while MMS supports multimedia content.\n\nAnswer: SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "gt": "The difference between SMS and MMS is that SMS allows you to send text-only messages of up to 160 characters, while MMS allows you to send messages with pictures, sounds, videos, and text, with a larger content size of up to 300KB.", "question": "what is the difference between sms and mms", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 31.562, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 57.69, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "737", "pred": "SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "raw_output": "SMS (Short Message Service) is a text messaging service that allows users to send text-only messages of up to 160 characters. Longer messages are automatically split into multiple parts. SMS is supported by most cell phones and is limited to plain text.\n\nMMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) is an evolution of SMS that allows users to send messages containing multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio files, and text in combination. MMS messages can carry larger content sizes, up to 300KB, and support formats like WAV, JPEG, and GIF.\n\nIn summary, the main difference is that SMS is limited to text, while MMS supports multimedia content.\n\nAnswer: SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "gt": "Based on the given passages, SMS stands for Short Message Service and allows you to send a message of up to 160 characters to another device. In contrast, MMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) allows you to send a message including pictures, videos, sounds, text, or any combination of the four. The content of an MMS message has a maximum size of 300KB in extensions of WAV, JPEG, GIF, etc.", "question": "what is the difference between sms and mms", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 31.645, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 36.62, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
{"id": "739", "pred": "SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "raw_output": "SMS (Short Message Service) is a text messaging service that allows users to send text-only messages of up to 160 characters. Longer messages are automatically split into multiple parts. SMS is supported by most cell phones and is limited to plain text.\n\nMMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) is an evolution of SMS that allows users to send messages containing multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio files, and text in combination. MMS messages can carry larger content sizes, up to 300KB, and support formats like WAV, JPEG, and GIF.\n\nIn summary, the main difference is that SMS is limited to text, while MMS supports multimedia content.\n\nAnswer: SMS is limited to sending text-only messages of up to 160 characters, whereas MMS allows sending multimedia content such as pictures, videos, audio, and text in combination, with support for larger file sizes up to 300KB.", "gt": "Sure! Here's my answer based on the given passages:\n\nThe main difference between SMS (Short Message Service) and MMS (Multimedia Messaging Service) is that SMS is limited to sending text-only messages, while MMS allows for sending multimedia content such as images, videos, and audio files, in addition to text.\n\nAccording to passage 1, SMS messages are limited to 160 characters and can be split into multiple parts if they are longer. MMS messages, on the other hand, can carry a larger size of content, including pictures, videos, and audio files, with a maximum size of 300KB.\n\nPassage 2 explains that SMS is a premium service offered by mobile companies, while passage 3 highlights the differences between SMS and MMS, stating that SMS only allows for text, while MMS allows for multimedia content.\n\nTherefore, the key differences between SMS and MMS are:\n\n* SMS is limited to text-only messages, while MMS allows for multimedia content.\n* SMS messages are limited to 160 characters and can be split into multiple parts, while MMS messages can carry a larger size of content.\n\nI hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions.", "question": "what is the difference between sms and mms", "group_key": "ragtruth", "sub_category": "ragtruth", "dataset": "ragtruth", "method": "vcd", "has_vcd": false, "time_sec": 31.513, "status": "success", "eval_metrics": {"Token-F1": 22.5, "GAVIE-Rel": -1.0, "GAVIE-Acc": -1.0, "Status": "success"}}
